module.exports = {
  id: 'azure', title: 'Microsoft Azure for DevOps Engineers', sub: 'From subscriptions and Entra ID to AKS, Functions, Monitor and cost, hands-on with the Azure CLI', cat: 'Cloud',
  accent: '#0078d4', icon: 'cloud', target: 90, keyword: 'AZURE', tags: '#azure #cloud #devops',
  scenes: [
    { type: 'hook', title: 'Microsoft Azure for DevOps Engineers', kicker: 'Cloud · 90 minutes', q: 'Why is the resource group your best friend in Azure?',
      n: 'Azure runs a huge share of enterprise workloads, and it connects deeply with the Microsoft tools many companies already use. In the next ninety minutes, we go from an empty subscription to networks, compute, containers, serverless, monitoring, security, and cost, typing real Azure CLI commands at every step. And you will see why the resource group is your best friend in Azure.' },
    { type: 'roadmap', n: 'Fifty steps in fifteen sections: the hierarchy, identity, governance, networking, compute, storage, databases, containers, app platforms, serverless and integration, infrastructure as code, CI and CD, observability, security and reliability, and cost.' },
    { type: 'step', x: 1, chip: 'Analogy', title: 'Azure is an office building', icon: 'layers',
      items: [['layers', 'Management groups = the company', 'Policies for every floor'], ['folder', 'Subscriptions = floors', 'Separate budgets and limits'], ['box', 'Resource groups = rooms', 'Things that live and die together'], ['key', 'Entra ID = the badge system', 'Who can open which door']],
      n: 'Picture an office building. Management groups are the company, setting rules for every floor. Subscriptions are floors, each with its own budget. Resource groups are rooms, holding things that live and die together. And Microsoft Entra ID is the badge system, deciding who can open which door.' },

    { sec: 'Hierarchy', short: 'Hierarchy', title: 'Tenant, management groups, subscriptions',
      nodes: [['users', 'Entra tenant', 'your organisation'], ['layers', 'Management groups', 'policy + RBAC'], ['folder', 'Subscriptions', 'billing + limits'], ['box', 'Resource groups'], ['server', 'Resources']], edges: ['', 'contain', 'contain', 'contain'],
      tip: 'Policies and permissions set high up are inherited by everything below.',
      n: 'Step one: the hierarchy. Everything starts with a Microsoft Entra tenant, your organisation. Management groups organise subscriptions, and subscriptions are the unit of billing and limits. Resource groups hold resources. Policies and permissions set at any level are inherited by everything below, which makes governance at scale much easier. Most organisations start with management groups for platform, landing zones, and sandbox, then place subscriptions underneath them by environment.' },
    { sec: 'Hierarchy', short: 'Regions & zones', title: 'Regions, availability zones and pairs',
      cmd: ['$ az account list-locations --query "[?metadata.regionType==\'Physical\'].name" -o tsv | head -n 4', 'eastus', 'westeurope', 'northeurope', 'centralindia', '$ az vm list-skus -l westeurope --zone --size Standard_D2s_v5 --query "[0].locationInfo[0].zones"'],
      tip: 'Use zone-redundant services, and paired regions for disaster recovery.',
      n: 'Step two: regions and zones. Azure has more than sixty regions. Most have three availability zones, which are separate data centres with independent power and networking. Many regions are paired with another region for disaster recovery. Use zone redundant services for anything important. Not every service and VM size is available in every region, so check availability and price before choosing a home region.' },
    { sec: 'Hierarchy', short: 'Azure CLI', title: 'The Azure CLI',
      cmd: ['$ az login', '$ az account set --subscription "Platform-Dev"', '$ az account show --query "{name:name, id:id, tenant:tenantId}" -o table', 'Name          Id                                    Tenant', 'Platform-Dev  1a2b3c4d-…                            9f8e7d6c-…', '$ az config set defaults.location=westeurope'],
      tip: '--query uses JMESPath; -o table, tsv or json shape the output.',
      n: 'Step three: the Azure CLI. az login opens a browser to sign in. az account set chooses the subscription, and account show confirms where you are. The query option filters output with JMESPath, and dash o sets the format. You can also set a default location, so you type less. The Azure Cloud Shell in the portal has the CLI preinstalled and already signed in, which is handy when you are away from your own machine.' },
    { sec: 'Hierarchy', short: 'Resource groups', title: 'Resource groups: your best friend',
      cmd: ['$ az group create --name rg-shop-dev --location westeurope --tags env=dev team=shop', '{ "name": "rg-shop-dev", "properties": { "provisioningState": "Succeeded" } }', '$ az resource list -g rg-shop-dev -o table', '$ az group delete --name rg-shop-dev --yes --no-wait'],
      tip: 'Group by lifecycle: everything in one group is deployed and deleted together.',
      n: 'Step four: the answer from the start. A resource group holds resources that share a lifecycle. Delete the group, and everything inside it is removed, with no forgotten pieces still costing money. It is also a natural boundary for permissions, policies, tags, and cost reports. Name groups consistently, like r g, dash, app, dash, environment. A resource can live in only one group, but it can still connect to resources in other groups, so organise by lifecycle, not by network.' },

    { sec: 'Identity', short: 'Entra ID', title: 'Microsoft Entra ID',
      items: [['users', 'Users and groups', 'Synced from on-prem or cloud-only'], ['lock', 'Conditional Access + MFA', 'Sign-in rules by risk and device'], ['key', 'App registrations', 'For apps that sign users in'], ['shield', 'Privileged Identity Management', 'Just-in-time admin access']],
      n: 'Step five: Microsoft Entra ID, formerly Azure Active Directory, is the identity service. It holds users and groups, often synced from an on-premises directory. Conditional Access enforces rules like multi-factor sign-in from unknown devices. And Privileged Identity Management grants admin rights just in time, for a limited period. Groups make access manageable: grant roles to groups, not individual users, and review membership every quarter with access reviews.' },
    { sec: 'Identity', short: 'RBAC', title: 'Azure RBAC: who can do what, where',
      cmd: ['$ az role assignment create --assignee devs@kona.com --role "Contributor" \\', '    --scope /subscriptions/$SUB/resourceGroups/rg-shop-dev', '$ az role assignment list -g rg-shop-dev --query "[].{who:principalName, role:roleDefinitionName}" -o table', 'Who             Role', 'devs@kona.com   Contributor'],
      tip: 'Owner, Contributor and Reader are the base roles. Prefer narrow built-in roles.',
      n: 'Step six: role based access control. A role assignment combines a principal, a role, and a scope. Here, the developers group gets Contributor on one resource group only. Owner, Contributor, and Reader are the broad base roles, but prefer narrower built-in roles, like AKS Cluster User, wherever they fit. Remember that Azure RBAC controls resources, while data access, like reading blobs or secrets, uses separate data roles.' },
    { sec: 'Identity', short: 'Managed identities', title: 'Managed identities: no secrets in code',
      cmd: ['$ az identity create -g rg-shop-dev -n id-shop-api', '$ az role assignment create --assignee-object-id $(az identity show -g rg-shop-dev -n id-shop-api --query principalId -o tsv) \\', '    --assignee-principal-type ServicePrincipal --role "Storage Blob Data Reader" --scope $STORAGE_ID', '# the app uses DefaultAzureCredential: no keys, no passwords'],
      tip: 'System-assigned identities live and die with one resource; user-assigned can be shared.',
      n: 'Step seven: managed identities give apps an identity in Entra without any secrets. Azure handles the credentials and rotates them. Create a user assigned identity, grant it a narrow data role, attach it to your app, and the SDK\'s DefaultAzureCredential uses it automatically. System assigned identities live and die with a single resource. This removes the most common source of leaked credentials: connection strings and keys copied into configuration files.' },
    { sec: 'Identity', short: 'Workload identity federation', title: 'CI without secrets: workload identity federation',
      cmd: ['$ az ad app federated-credential create --id $APP_ID --parameters \'{', '    "name": "gh-main", "issuer": "https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com",', '    "subject": "repo:kona/shop:ref:refs/heads/main", "audiences": ["api://AzureADTokenExchange"] }\'', '# GitHub Actions and Azure Pipelines exchange OIDC tokens; no client secret is stored'],
      n: 'Step eight: CI systems should not store client secrets. Workload identity federation lets GitHub Actions, Azure Pipelines, or Kubernetes exchange their own OIDC tokens for Azure access tokens. The subject limits trust to one repository and branch, so nothing else can use it. For Kubernetes, the same federation powers AKS workload identity, so pods get tokens without any secrets mounted into them.' },
    { sec: 'Identity', short: 'Conditional Access', title: 'Conditional Access and PIM', items: [['shield', 'Conditional Access', 'MFA, compliant device, location'], ['clock', 'Privileged Identity Management', 'Just-in-time admin, with approval'], ['users', 'Access reviews', 'Owners re-approve every quarter'], ['key', 'Phishing-resistant MFA', 'Passkeys and FIDO2 for admins']],
      tip: 'Nobody should hold Owner permanently. PIM makes admin rights expire after a few hours.',
      n: 'Step nine: Conditional Access decides how people may sign in: require MFA, a compliant device, or a trusted location. Privileged Identity Management makes admin roles just in time, activated for a few hours with approval and a reason. Access reviews make owners re approve who still needs access. And for admins, use phishing resistant MFA, like passkeys.' },
    { sec: 'Identity', short: 'RBAC scopes', title: 'RBAC scopes and custom roles', cols: ['Scope', 'Example assignment', 'Inherited by'],
      rows: [['Management group', 'Security Reader for the security team', 'Every subscription below'], ['Subscription', 'Reader for auditors', 'All resource groups'], ['Resource group', 'Contributor for the shop team', 'All resources in it'], ['Resource', 'Key Vault Secrets User for one app', 'Only that vault']],
      tip: 'Assign at the narrowest scope that works. Use custom roles to remove dangerous actions.',
      n: 'Step ten: RBAC assignments inherit downwards. Assign at a management group, and every subscription below gets it. A subscription, and every resource group in it. A resource group, and all its resources. Or a single resource, like one key vault. Always choose the narrowest scope that works, and create custom roles to remove dangerous actions, such as deleting locks.' },

    { sec: 'Governance', short: 'Azure Policy', title: 'Guardrails with Azure Policy',
      cmd: ['$ az policy assignment create --name allowed-locations \\', '    --policy e56962a6-4747-49cd-b67b-bf8b01975c4c \\', '    --params \'{"listOfAllowedLocations":{"value":["westeurope","northeurope"]}}\' \\', '    --scope /providers/Microsoft.Management/managementGroups/kona-prod', '$ az policy state summarize --management-group kona-prod --query "results.nonCompliantResources"'],
      tip: 'Effects: Deny, Audit, Modify and DeployIfNotExists.',
      n: 'Step eleven: Azure Policy enforces rules across your estate. This assignment allows resources only in two European regions, for every subscription under the production management group. Effects can deny, audit, modify resources, or deploy missing settings automatically, and compliance is reported continuously. Group related policies into initiatives, like the Microsoft cloud security benchmark, and start in audit mode before switching to deny.' },
    { sec: 'Governance', short: 'Policy effects', title: 'Policy effects: audit, deny, modify, deploy', cols: ['Effect', 'What it does', 'Example'],
      rows: [['Audit', 'Reports non-compliance only', 'Storage without private endpoint'], ['Deny', 'Blocks the create or update', 'Public IP on a NIC'], ['Modify', 'Adds or changes properties', 'Add the cost-center tag'], ['DeployIfNotExists', 'Deploys a missing companion', 'Diagnostic settings to Log Analytics']],
      tip: 'Start with Audit, fix the findings, then switch to Deny. Remediation tasks fix existing resources.',
      n: 'Step twelve: policy effects. Audit only reports. Deny blocks the request, for example a public IP on a network interface. Modify adds or changes properties, like a missing tag. And deploy if not exists creates a companion resource, such as diagnostic settings sending logs to Log Analytics. Start in audit, fix what it finds, then switch to deny.' },
    { sec: 'Governance', short: 'Landing zone', title: 'The Azure landing zone hierarchy', nodes: [['layers', 'Tenant root group', 'baseline policy'], ['shield', 'Platform', 'identity, connectivity'], ['server', 'Landing zones', 'corp (private), online (public)'], ['folder', 'Sandbox / decommissioned', 'loose rules / locked']], edges: ['', '', ''],
      tip: 'The Azure Landing Zones accelerator (Bicep or Terraform) builds this hierarchy with policies.',
      n: 'Step thirteen: the landing zone hierarchy. Under the tenant root, a platform group holds identity, management, and connectivity subscriptions. A landing zones group splits into corp, for private apps connected to the hub, and online, for internet facing apps. Sandbox has loose rules for experiments, and decommissioned locks old subscriptions. The landing zones accelerator builds all of it with code.' },
    { sec: 'Governance', short: 'Tags, locks, landing zones', title: 'Tags, locks and landing zones',
      items: [['tag', 'Tags: env, team, cost-center', 'Enforce them with policy'], ['lock', 'Resource locks', 'CanNotDelete on critical resources'], ['layers', 'Azure landing zones', 'A proven, scalable starting design'], ['map', 'Hub-and-spoke or Virtual WAN', 'Shared network services']],
      n: 'Step fourteen: governance basics. Tag everything with environment, team, and cost centre, and require tags with policy. Put a delete lock on critical resources, like production databases. And start large estates from the Azure landing zone design, with a hub and spoke network, or Virtual WAN.' },

    { sec: 'Networking', short: 'VNets & subnets', title: 'Virtual networks and subnets',
      cmd: ['$ az network vnet create -g rg-shop-dev -n vnet-shop --address-prefix 10.20.0.0/16 \\', '    --subnet-name snet-app --subnet-prefixes 10.20.1.0/24', '$ az network vnet subnet create -g rg-shop-dev --vnet-name vnet-shop -n snet-data --address-prefixes 10.20.2.0/24', '$ az network vnet subnet list -g rg-shop-dev --vnet-name vnet-shop -o table'],
      tip: 'Subnets span all zones in a region. Azure reserves 5 IPs in each subnet.',
      n: 'Step fifteen: a virtual network, or VNet, is your private network in a region. Subnets divide it, and unlike some clouds, an Azure subnet spans all availability zones in the region. Azure reserves five addresses in every subnet. Plan address ranges carefully, so they never overlap with other networks you may connect later. Some services need a dedicated subnet, delegated to them, so leave spare address space for future growth.' },
    { sec: 'Networking', short: 'NSGs', title: 'Network security groups',
      cmd: ['$ az network nsg create -g rg-shop-dev -n nsg-app', '$ az network nsg rule create -g rg-shop-dev --nsg-name nsg-app -n allow-https \\', '    --priority 100 --direction Inbound --access Allow --protocol Tcp --destination-port-ranges 443', '$ az network vnet subnet update -g rg-shop-dev --vnet-name vnet-shop -n snet-app --network-security-group nsg-app'],
      tip: 'Lower priority numbers win. Use application security groups to name groups of VMs.',
      n: 'Step sixteen: network security groups are stateful firewalls for subnets and network interfaces. Rules have priorities, and the lowest number wins. Here, we allow HTTPS inbound and attach the group to the app subnet. Application security groups let you write rules by role, like web servers, instead of by IP address. Turn on VNet flow logs, and use Network Watcher to troubleshoot which rule is blocking a connection.' },
    { sec: 'Networking', short: 'Connectivity', title: 'Connect networks privately',
      items: [['link', 'VNet peering', 'Low-latency link between VNets'], ['lock', 'Private Endpoints', 'PaaS services on a private IP'], ['route', 'VPN Gateway · ExpressRoute', 'Link your data centre'], ['shield', 'Azure Firewall · NAT Gateway', 'Central egress control']],
      n: 'Step seventeen: private connectivity. VNet peering joins networks with low latency. Private endpoints give platform services, like storage and databases, a private IP inside your VNet, so traffic never crosses the internet. VPN Gateway and ExpressRoute connect your data centre. And Azure Firewall with a NAT gateway controls outbound traffic centrally. A common pattern is hub and spoke: shared firewall, VPN, and DNS in a hub VNet, and each application in its own peered spoke.' },
    { sec: 'Networking', short: 'Load balancing', title: 'Load balancing options', cols: ['Layer / scope', 'Use it for'],
      rows: [['Load Balancer', 'L4, regional', 'TCP/UDP to VMs'], ['Application Gateway', 'L7, regional + WAF', 'Web apps, path routing'], ['Front Door', 'L7, global + CDN + WAF', 'Global web apps'], ['Traffic Manager', 'DNS-based, global', 'Failover between regions']],
      n: 'Step eighteen: load balancing. Azure Load Balancer works at layer four inside a region. Application Gateway works at layer seven, with path routing and a web application firewall. Front Door is a global layer seven entry point with caching and protection. And Traffic Manager steers users between regions with DNS.' },
    { sec: 'Networking', short: 'DNS', title: 'Azure DNS and private DNS zones',
      cmd: ['$ az network dns record-set a add-record -g rg-dns -z kona.com -n shop -a 20.50.10.7', '$ az network private-dns zone create -g rg-shop-dev -n privatelink.blob.core.windows.net', '$ az network private-dns link vnet create -g rg-shop-dev -z privatelink.blob.core.windows.net \\', '    -n link-shop -v vnet-shop -e false'],
      tip: 'Private endpoints need private DNS zones, or names resolve to public IPs.',
      n: 'Step nineteen: DNS. Azure DNS hosts your public zones. Private DNS zones resolve names inside your VNets, and they are essential for private endpoints: link the private link zone to your VNet, otherwise service names still resolve to public addresses.' },
    { sec: 'Networking deep dive', short: 'Address planning', title: 'Plan VNet address space', cols: ['Range', 'Use', 'Note'],
      rows: [['10.0.0.0/16', 'Hub VNet', 'Firewall, gateway, Bastion subnets'], ['10.10.0.0/16', 'Spoke: shop prod', 'Never overlaps another spoke'], ['/24 subnet', 'Apps, private endpoints', 'Azure reserves 5 addresses'], ['Firewall subnet /26', 'Exact name required', 'Also Gateway, Bastion subnets']],
      tip: 'Some subnets need exact names. Use Azure Virtual Network Manager IPAM pools to avoid overlaps.',
      n: 'Step twenty: plan the address space. Give the hub and each spoke its own range that never overlaps, because peering cannot connect overlapping networks. Azure reserves five addresses in every subnet. Some subnets need exact names: AzureFirewallSubnet, GatewaySubnet, and AzureBastionSubnet. Virtual Network Manager IP address pools can hand out ranges automatically.' },
    { sec: 'Networking deep dive', short: 'Routing', title: 'System routes and user-defined routes',
      cmd: ['$ az network route-table create -g rg-net -n rt-spoke-shop', '$ az network route-table route create -g rg-net --route-table-name rt-spoke-shop -n default \\', '    --address-prefix 0.0.0.0/0 --next-hop-type VirtualAppliance --next-hop-ip-address 10.0.1.4', '$ az network vnet subnet update -g rg-net --vnet-name vnet-shop -n snet-app --route-table rt-spoke-shop', '$ az network nic show-effective-route-table -g rg-shop -n vm-web-01VMNic -o table'],
      tip: 'show-effective-route-table shows what a NIC really uses: system, BGP and your UDRs combined.',
      n: 'Step twenty one: routing. Azure creates system routes automatically, so subnets talk to each other and reach the internet. A user defined route overrides them: here, all traffic, zero dot zero dot zero dot zero slash zero, goes to the firewall at ten dot zero dot one dot four. Attach the route table to the subnet. And show effective route table tells you exactly which routes a network interface really uses.' },
    { sec: 'Networking deep dive', short: 'NSG + ASG', title: 'NSG rules with application security groups',
      cmd: ['$ az network asg create -g rg-shop -n asg-web && az network asg create -g rg-shop -n asg-db', '$ az network nsg rule create -g rg-shop --nsg-name nsg-data -n allow-web-to-db --priority 100 \\', '    --source-asgs asg-web --destination-asgs asg-db --destination-port-ranges 5432 --access Allow', '$ az network nsg rule create -g rg-shop --nsg-name nsg-data -n deny-all-in --priority 4000 \\', '    --source-address-prefixes "*" --destination-port-ranges "*" --access Deny'],
      tip: 'ASGs group NICs by role, so rules say "web to db" instead of listing IP addresses.',
      n: 'Step twenty two: network security groups with application security groups. Group network interfaces by role, web and database, then write rules between groups: web may reach the database on port fifty four thirty two. Lower priority numbers win. Add an explicit deny at the end so intent is obvious. Because rules reference groups, scaling out never requires a rule change.' },
    { sec: 'Networking deep dive', short: 'Private endpoints', title: 'Private endpoints and private DNS',
      cmd: ['$ az network private-endpoint create -g rg-shop -n pe-sql --vnet-name vnet-shop --subnet snet-pe \\', '    --private-connection-resource-id $SQL_ID --group-id sqlServer --connection-name sql', '$ az network private-dns zone create -g rg-dns -n privatelink.database.windows.net', '$ az network private-endpoint dns-zone-group create -g rg-shop --endpoint-name pe-sql -n default \\', '    --private-dns-zone privatelink.database.windows.net --zone-name sql', '$ nslookup sql-shop.database.windows.net   # → 10.10.2.5'],
      tip: 'The DNS zone group is what makes the public name resolve to the private IP. Forget it and apps still go public.',
      n: 'Step twenty three: private endpoints. A private endpoint puts a PaaS service, like Azure SQL, onto a private IP inside your VNet. The step people forget is DNS: link the private link DNS zone with a zone group, so the normal name, sql shop dot database dot windows dot net, resolves to ten dot ten dot two dot five. Then disable public network access on the service itself.' },
    { sec: 'Networking deep dive', short: 'Service endpoints vs PE', title: 'Service endpoints vs private endpoints', cols: ['', 'Service endpoint', 'Private endpoint'],
      rows: [['Traffic path', 'Azure backbone, public IP of service', 'Private IP in your subnet'], ['Reach from on-prem', 'No', 'Yes, over VPN or ExpressRoute'], ['Data exfiltration', 'Any account of that type', 'Only that one resource'], ['Cost', 'Free', 'Per hour and per GB']],
      tip: 'Private endpoints are the secure default. Service endpoints are a cheap step for simple cases.',
      n: 'Step twenty four: service endpoints versus private endpoints. A service endpoint keeps traffic on the Azure backbone, but the service keeps its public address, it does not work from on premises, and it allows any account of that type. A private endpoint gives one specific resource a private IP, reachable from on premises, which blocks data exfiltration to other accounts. It costs a little, but it is the secure default.' },
    { sec: 'Networking deep dive', short: 'Egress', title: 'Outbound traffic: NAT Gateway and default outbound',
      items: [['globe', 'NAT Gateway', 'Static outbound IPs, no SNAT exhaustion'], ['x', 'Default outbound access is retired', 'New VNets: subnets are private by default'], ['shield', 'Azure Firewall as egress', 'UDR 0.0.0.0/0 to the firewall'], ['dollar', 'Watch data processing charges', 'Private endpoints avoid some egress']],
      tip: 'Since 2025, new subnets have no implicit internet access. Plan NAT Gateway or a firewall explicitly.',
      n: 'Step twenty five: outbound traffic. NAT Gateway gives a subnet static outbound IPs, and removes source NAT port exhaustion. Default outbound access has been retired for new virtual networks, so new subnets are private by default, and you must choose an explicit path: a NAT gateway, or a user defined route sending everything to Azure Firewall. That explicit choice is good for security.' },
    { sec: 'Networking deep dive', short: 'Hub and spoke', title: 'Hub and spoke with VNet peering',
      nodes: [['server', 'Spoke: shop', '10.10.0.0/16'], ['link', 'Peering', 'use remote gateway'], ['shield', 'Hub', 'Firewall, Bastion, VPN/ER'], ['server', 'Spoke: billing', '10.11.0.0/16']], edges: ['', 'UDR via firewall', ''],
      tip: 'Peering is not transitive. Spoke-to-spoke traffic must route through the hub firewall.',
      n: 'Step twenty six: hub and spoke. Each spoke VNet peers with the hub, which holds the firewall, Bastion, and the VPN or ExpressRoute gateway. Spokes use the hub gateway to reach on premises. Peering is not transitive, so spoke to spoke traffic needs a user defined route through the hub firewall, which is exactly where you want to inspect it.' },
    { sec: 'Networking deep dive', short: 'Virtual WAN & AVNM', title: 'Virtual WAN and Virtual Network Manager', cols: ['Service', 'Gives you', 'Choose when'],
      rows: [['Virtual WAN', 'Microsoft-managed hubs, any-to-any', 'Many regions, branches, SD-WAN'], ['Network Manager', 'Admin rules at scale', 'Hundreds of VNets'], ['Security admin rules', 'Rules evaluated BEFORE every NSG', 'Org-wide must-deny ports'], ['IPAM pools', 'Non-overlapping ranges', 'Self-service VNets']],
      tip: 'Security admin rules cannot be overridden by application teams, unlike NSGs.',
      n: 'Step twenty seven: networking at scale. Virtual WAN gives Microsoft managed hubs with any to any routing between regions, branches, and VNets. Virtual Network Manager manages hundreds of VNets centrally: it builds hub and spoke or mesh connectivity, and applies security admin rules, which are evaluated before every NSG, so app teams cannot override must deny rules like blocking RDP from the internet.' },
    { sec: 'Networking deep dive', short: 'Azure Firewall', title: 'Azure Firewall Premium and policies',
      items: [['shield', 'Rule collections', 'DNAT, network, application (FQDN)'], ['search', 'IDPS', 'Signature-based intrusion detection'], ['lock', 'TLS inspection', 'Decrypt and inspect outbound HTTPS'], ['eye', 'Threat intelligence', 'Alert or deny known bad IPs and domains']],
      cmd: ['$ az network firewall policy create -g rg-net -n afwp-hub --sku Premium \\', '    --threat-intel-mode Deny --idps-mode Deny', '$ az network firewall policy rule-collection-group collection add-filter-collection \\', '    -g rg-net --policy-name afwp-hub --rcg-name app --name allow-github --action Allow \\', '    --rule-type ApplicationRule --target-fqdns api.github.com --protocols Https=443 --priority 200'],
      n: 'Step twenty eight: Azure Firewall. Rules live in a firewall policy, organised into collections: destination NAT, network rules, and application rules that match fully qualified domain names. The Premium tier adds intrusion detection and prevention, TLS inspection of outbound HTTPS, and threat intelligence that denies known bad addresses. Here, outbound HTTPS is allowed only to the GitHub API.' },
    { sec: 'Networking deep dive', short: 'Bastion', title: 'Azure Bastion: no public IPs on VMs',
      cmd: ['$ az network bastion create -g rg-net -n bas-hub --vnet-name vnet-hub \\', '    --sku Standard --enable-tunneling true --session-recording false', '$ az network bastion ssh -g rg-net -n bas-hub --target-resource-id $VM_ID \\', '    --auth-type AAD', 'azureuser@vm-web-01:~$'],
      tip: 'With Entra ID login, there are no SSH keys to manage. Premium adds session recording.',
      n: 'Step twenty nine: Azure Bastion. Virtual machines need no public IP and no open SSH or RDP port. Bastion, in the hub, opens sessions from the portal or the CLI, here SSH with Microsoft Entra authentication, so there are no keys to manage. Tunneling enables native clients, and the premium tier records sessions for audits.' },
    { sec: 'Networking deep dive', short: 'Hybrid', title: 'VPN Gateway and ExpressRoute', cols: ['Option', 'Path', 'Choose when'],
      rows: [['Site-to-site VPN', 'IPsec over the internet', 'Quick start, backup path'], ['Point-to-site VPN', 'Per user, Entra ID auth', 'Remote engineers'], ['ExpressRoute', 'Private circuit, up to 100 Gbps', 'Production hybrid traffic'], ['ExpressRoute + VPN', 'Private plus encrypted failover', 'Critical workloads']],
      tip: 'Use zone-redundant gateway SKUs (AZ) and two ExpressRoute circuits in different peering locations.',
      n: 'Step thirty: connecting on premises. Site to site VPN runs IPsec tunnels over the internet, quick to set up and a good backup. Point to site VPN connects individual engineers, authenticated with Entra ID. ExpressRoute is a private circuit with steady latency for production. For critical workloads, combine ExpressRoute with a VPN failover, and use zone redundant gateway sizes.' },
    { sec: 'Networking deep dive', short: 'Private DNS Resolver', title: 'Hybrid DNS with Private DNS Resolver',
      nodes: [['server', 'On-prem DNS', 'corp.contoso'], ['route', 'Inbound endpoint', '10.0.5.4'], ['cloud', 'Azure DNS', 'private zones'], ['route', 'Outbound endpoint', 'forwarding ruleset']], edges: ['conditional forward', '', 'corp.contoso → on-prem'],
      tip: 'Replace DNS forwarder VMs with the managed resolver. Link forwarding rulesets to every spoke.',
      n: 'Step thirty one: hybrid DNS. Azure DNS Private Resolver replaces the DNS forwarder virtual machines people used to run. Its inbound endpoint lets on premises servers resolve your private zones, including private endpoint names. Its outbound endpoint, with a forwarding ruleset, sends queries for corp dot contoso to your data centre. Link the ruleset to every spoke.' },
    { sec: 'Networking deep dive', short: 'Encryption & logs', title: 'VNet encryption and flow logs',
      cmd: ['$ az network vnet update -g rg-shop -n vnet-shop --enable-encryption true \\', '    --encryption-enforcement-policy AllowUnencrypted', '$ az network watcher flow-log create -g rg-net -n fl-vnet-shop --location westeurope \\', '    --vnet vnet-shop --storage-account stflowlogs8842 --traffic-analytics true \\', '    --workspace $LAW_ID'],
      tip: 'VNet flow logs replaced NSG flow logs. Traffic Analytics turns them into maps and top talkers.',
      n: 'Step thirty two: encryption and visibility. Virtual network encryption encrypts traffic between virtual machines inside a VNet, with an enforcement policy that can drop unencrypted traffic later. VNet flow logs, which replaced NSG flow logs, record every flow. Turn on Traffic Analytics with a Log Analytics workspace, and you get maps, top talkers, and blocked flows without writing queries.' },
    { sec: 'Networking deep dive', short: 'Troubleshoot', title: 'Network Watcher troubleshooting',
      items: [['search', 'IP flow verify', 'Which NSG rule allows or denies?'], ['map', 'Next hop', 'Where will this packet go?'], ['link', 'Connection troubleshoot', 'End-to-end test with latency'], ['eye', 'Packet capture', 'Record traffic on a VM']],
      n: 'Step thirty three: when something cannot connect, Network Watcher answers it. IP flow verify tells you which NSG rule allows or denies a packet. Next hop shows where a packet will go: the internet, a peering, or the firewall. Connection troubleshoot tests end to end with latency at each hop. And packet capture records the traffic itself for deep analysis.' },
    { sec: 'Networking deep dive', short: 'Edge', title: 'Front Door, Application Gateway and WAF', cols: ['Service', 'Scope', 'Use for'],
      rows: [['Front Door Premium', 'Global edge, anycast', 'CDN, global routing, WAF'], ['Application Gateway v2', 'Regional, in your VNet', 'L7 routing, WAF, private apps'], ['AGC (containers)', 'Regional, for AKS', 'Gateway API ingress'], ['Load Balancer Standard', 'Regional L4', 'TCP/UDP, internal services']],
      tip: 'Front Door Premium can reach origins over Private Link, so the app needs no public endpoint at all.',
      n: 'Step thirty four: the edge. Front Door Premium is a global entry point at Microsoft edge locations, with caching, routing between regions, WAF, and origins reached over Private Link, so the app itself needs no public endpoint. Application Gateway is a regional layer seven load balancer with WAF, inside your VNet. Application Gateway for Containers serves AKS through the Gateway API. Standard Load Balancer handles layer four.' },
    { sec: 'Networking deep dive', short: 'DDoS', title: 'DDoS protection', items: [['shield', 'Infrastructure protection', 'Free, always on, platform level'], ['gauge', 'DDoS IP Protection', 'Per public IP, adaptive tuning'], ['users', 'DDoS Network Protection', 'Per VNet, rapid response, cost guarantee'], ['eye', 'Metrics and alerts', 'Under DDoS attack or not']],
      n: 'Step thirty five: DDoS protection. Every public IP gets basic infrastructure protection at no cost. DDoS IP Protection adds adaptive tuning for individual public IPs. DDoS Network Protection covers whole virtual networks, with access to the rapid response team and credits for scale out costs during an attack. Alert on the under DDoS attack metric.' },
    { sec: 'DNS deep dive', short: 'Zones and records', title: 'Public zones, alias records and delegation',
      cmd: ['$ az network dns zone create -g rg-dns -n contoso-shop.com', '$ az network dns zone show -g rg-dns -n contoso-shop.com --query nameServers', '[ "ns1-07.azure-dns.com.", "ns2-07.azure-dns.net.", … ]', '$ az network dns record-set a create -g rg-dns -z contoso-shop.com -n www \\', '    --target-resource $FRONT_DOOR_ENDPOINT_ID   # alias record'],
      tip: 'Alias records follow the target, so an IP change never breaks DNS. Lock the zone against deletion.',
      n: 'Step thirty six: public DNS. Create the zone, then delegate it by setting the four Azure name servers at your registrar. Alias record sets point directly at Azure resources, such as a Front Door endpoint or a public IP, and follow them automatically. Put a delete lock on production zones, because deleting a zone takes the whole domain offline.' },
    { sec: 'DNS deep dive', short: 'Private zones', title: 'Private DNS zones and links', items: [['link', 'Virtual network links', 'Which VNets can resolve the zone'], ['refresh', 'Auto-registration', 'VM names registered automatically'], ['lock', 'privatelink.* zones', 'One per PaaS service, central in the hub'], ['layers', 'Fallback to internet', 'Resolve public names if no record']],
      tip: 'Keep privatelink zones in one central subscription and link them to every spoke.',
      n: 'Step thirty seven: private DNS zones. A zone answers only for the virtual networks linked to it. Auto registration adds virtual machine names automatically. Keep every privatelink zone, one per PaaS service, in one central connectivity subscription, linked to every spoke, so private endpoint names resolve the same everywhere. Fallback to internet resolution helps when a private record does not exist.' },
    { sec: 'DNS deep dive', short: 'Traffic Manager', title: 'Traffic Manager and Front Door routing', cols: ['Routing method', 'Sends users to'],
      rows: [['Priority', 'Primary, then failover endpoint'], ['Weighted', 'Split by percentage'], ['Performance', 'Lowest-latency region'], ['Geographic', 'By country or region'], ['Front Door origin groups', 'Health-probed, latency-aware, with session affinity']],
      tip: 'Traffic Manager works at DNS level; Front Door proxies every request, so failover is faster.',
      n: 'Step thirty eight: global routing. Traffic Manager is DNS based, with priority failover, weighted splits, performance routing to the closest region, and geographic routing. Front Door does the same with real health probes on every request, because it proxies traffic instead of only answering DNS, so failover takes seconds rather than waiting for DNS caches to expire.' },
    { sec: 'DNS deep dive', short: 'DNS security', title: 'DNS security: DNSSEC and DNS security policy',
      items: [['lock', 'DNSSEC for public zones', 'Signed answers, DS record at registrar'], ['shield', 'DNS security policy', 'Block or alert on domains per VNet'], ['list', 'DNS query logs', 'To Log Analytics for investigations'], ['key', 'Registrar lock', 'Stop hijacking and expiry']],
      n: 'Step thirty nine: securing DNS. Turn on DNSSEC signing for public zones, and publish the D S record at your registrar. A DNS security policy filters queries from your virtual networks, blocking or alerting on malicious domains, and sends query logs to Log Analytics, so during an incident you can see which machine looked up which domain. And lock your domains at the registrar.' },

    { sec: 'Compute', short: 'Virtual machines', title: 'Virtual machines',
      cmd: ['$ az vm create -g rg-shop-dev -n vm-web-01 --image Ubuntu2404 --size Standard_B2s \\', '    --vnet-name vnet-shop --subnet snet-app --zone 1 --assign-identity \\', '    --admin-username azureuser --generate-ssh-keys --public-ip-address ""', '$ az vm list -g rg-shop-dev -d --query "[].{name:name, state:powerState, ip:privateIps}" -o table', 'vm-web-01   VM running   10.20.1.4'],
      tip: 'No public IP: connect with Azure Bastion instead of exposing SSH or RDP.',
      n: 'Step forty: virtual machines. az vm create builds one from an image, in a zone, with a managed identity and SSH keys, and here with no public IP. Use Azure Bastion to connect through the portal or CLI, so SSH and RDP are never exposed to the internet. Use cloud init or a custom image to configure VMs on first boot, and prefer replacing VMs over patching them by hand.' },
    { sec: 'Compute', short: 'VM sizes & pricing', title: 'VM families and pricing models', cols: ['Good for', 'Saves'],
      rows: [['Pay-as-you-go', 'Short or spiky workloads', 'Nothing: full price'], ['Reservations', 'Steady 24/7 usage', 'Up to ~65–72%'], ['Spot VMs', 'Interruptible batch and CI', 'Up to ~90%'], ['Azure Hybrid Benefit', 'Existing Windows/SQL licences', 'Licence cost']],
      tip: 'B = burstable · D = general · E = memory · F = compute · N = GPU.',
      n: 'Step forty one: pricing. Pay as you go is full price, for short or spiky workloads. Reservations and the savings plan for compute cut steady workloads significantly. Spot VMs use spare capacity at deep discounts, but can be evicted. And Azure Hybrid Benefit reuses existing Windows Server and SQL Server licences.' },
    { sec: 'Compute', short: 'Scale sets', title: 'Virtual Machine Scale Sets',
      cmd: ['$ az vmss create -g rg-shop-dev -n vmss-web --image Ubuntu2404 --instance-count 2 \\', '    --vnet-name vnet-shop --subnet snet-app --zones 1 2 3 --orchestration-mode Flexible \\', '    --admin-username azureuser --generate-ssh-keys', '$ az monitor autoscale create -g rg-shop-dev --resource vmss-web --resource-type Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachineScaleSets \\', '    --name as-web --min-count 2 --max-count 10 --count 2'],
      n: 'Step forty two: scale sets run a fleet of identical VMs across zones, and replace unhealthy ones. Autoscale rules add instances when CPU is high and remove them when it is low, between two and ten here. Flexible orchestration is the recommended mode for new scale sets. Automatic OS image upgrades and rolling upgrade policies keep the fleet patched, a few instances at a time.' },
    { sec: 'Compute deep dive', short: 'VM names', title: 'Read a VM size: Standard_D4pds_v6', items: [['tag', 'D = family', 'general purpose (E memory, F compute, N GPU)'], ['hash', '4 = vCPUs', 'size within the family'], ['cpu', 'p = Arm (Cobalt)', 'a AMD, d local disk, s premium storage'], ['refresh', 'v6 = version', 'newer is faster per dollar']],
      tip: 'Cobalt 100 Arm VMs (Dpsv6, Epsv6) give strong price-performance for Linux workloads.',
      n: 'Step forty three: read a VM size. In Standard D four p d s version six, D is the family, general purpose; E is memory optimised, F compute optimised, and N has GPUs. Four is the number of vCPUs. p means an Arm processor, Microsoft Cobalt; a means AMD, d a local temporary disk, and s premium storage support. And v six is the generation.' },
    { sec: 'Compute deep dive', short: 'Trusted launch', title: 'Trusted launch, encryption at host and updates', items: [['shield', 'Trusted launch', 'Secure Boot + vTPM, default for new VMs'], ['lock', 'Encryption at host', 'Temp disk and caches encrypted too'], ['refresh', 'Azure Update Manager', 'Patch schedules across VMs and Arc servers'], ['key', 'Entra ID login for VMs', 'No local accounts or SSH keys']],
      n: 'Step forty four: harden every VM. Trusted launch, now the default, adds Secure Boot and a virtual TPM against boot kits. Encryption at host also encrypts the temporary disk and caches. Azure Update Manager schedules patching across Azure VMs and Arc connected servers. And Entra ID login replaces local accounts and SSH keys, with role based access to the VM itself.' },
    { sec: 'Compute deep dive', short: 'Scale sets deep', title: 'Scale sets: Flexible orchestration and Spot', items: [['layers', 'Flexible orchestration', 'Mix sizes, zones, Spot and regular'], ['refresh', 'Rolling upgrades + health probe', 'Automatic OS image upgrades'], ['zap', 'Spot priority mix', 'For example 20% regular, 80% Spot'], ['gauge', 'Autoscale on metrics or schedule', 'Predictive autoscale too']],
      n: 'Step forty five: scale sets in depth. Flexible orchestration lets one scale set mix VM sizes, zones, and Spot with regular capacity, for example twenty percent regular and eighty percent Spot. Rolling upgrades use a health probe, so new images roll out safely. Autoscale works on metrics, on schedules, or predictively, learning daily patterns.' },

    { sec: 'Storage', short: 'Storage accounts', title: 'Storage accounts and Blob storage',
      cmd: ['$ az storage account create -g rg-shop-dev -n stshopdev8842 --sku Standard_ZRS \\', '    --kind StorageV2 --min-tls-version TLS1_2 --allow-blob-public-access false', '$ az storage container create --account-name stshopdev8842 -n releases --auth-mode login', '$ az storage blob upload --account-name stshopdev8842 -c releases -f app.tgz -n 1.4.2/app.tgz --auth-mode login'],
      tip: '--auth-mode login uses your Entra identity instead of account keys.',
      n: 'Step forty six: a storage account holds blobs, files, queues, and tables. Choose redundancy, here zone redundant, require modern TLS, and disable public blob access. Upload with auth mode login, which uses your Entra identity and RBAC instead of the powerful account keys. Better still, disable shared key access entirely, so only Entra identities can reach the data.' },
    { sec: 'Storage', short: 'Tiers & lifecycle', title: 'Redundancy, access tiers and lifecycle',
      items: [['refresh', 'LRS · ZRS · GRS · GZRS', 'Copies in a zone, zones, or regions'], ['zap', 'Hot · Cool · Cold · Archive', 'Pay less for rarely-read data'], ['trash', 'Lifecycle management rules', 'Move or delete blobs by age'], ['shield', 'Soft delete and versioning', 'Recover from mistakes']],
      n: 'Step forty seven: protect and optimise data. Redundancy options range from locally redundant to geo zone redundant. Access tiers, from hot to archive, lower the cost of data you rarely read. Lifecycle rules move or delete blobs by age. And soft delete with versioning lets you recover from accidental deletes.' },
    { sec: 'Storage', short: 'Disks & files', title: 'Managed disks and Azure Files', cols: ['What it is', 'Use it for'],
      rows: [['Managed disks', 'Block storage for one VM', 'OS and data disks'], ['Premium SSD v2 / Ultra', 'High IOPS, low latency', 'Databases'], ['Azure Files', 'SMB and NFS shares', 'Lift-and-shift, shared config'], ['Azure NetApp Files', 'Enterprise NFS/SMB', 'Demanding file workloads']],
      n: 'Step forty eight: managed disks are block storage for VMs, with Premium SSD version two and Ultra Disk for demanding databases. Azure Files offers managed SMB and NFS shares, great for lift and shift apps and shared configuration. Azure NetApp Files serves the most demanding file workloads.' },
    { sec: 'Storage deep dive', short: 'Secure account', title: 'A secure storage account',
      cmd: ['$ az storage account create -g rg-shop -n stshopprod8842 --sku Standard_ZRS --kind StorageV2 \\', '    --min-tls-version TLS1_2 --allow-blob-public-access false \\', '    --allow-shared-key-access false --public-network-access Disabled \\', '    --allow-cross-tenant-replication false --https-only true', '$ az role assignment create --assignee-object-id $APP_MI --role "Storage Blob Data Reader" --scope $SA_ID'],
      tip: 'Disable shared keys: then only Entra ID identities with data roles can read data.',
      n: 'Step forty nine: a secure storage account. Zone redundant, TLS one point two minimum, no anonymous blob access, and, most important, shared key access disabled, so account keys and key based SAS tokens stop working, and only Entra ID identities with data roles can read data. Disable public network access and use a private endpoint. Grant apps the Storage Blob Data Reader role through managed identities.' },
    { sec: 'Storage deep dive', short: 'Network rules', title: 'Firewall rules and private endpoints per service', cols: ['Sub-resource', 'Private DNS zone'],
      rows: [['blob', 'privatelink.blob.core.windows.net'], ['dfs (Data Lake)', 'privatelink.dfs.core.windows.net'], ['file', 'privatelink.file.core.windows.net'], ['queue / table', 'privatelink.queue… / privatelink.table…']],
      tip: 'Each storage service needs its own private endpoint and DNS zone. Blob alone does not cover Files.',
      n: 'Step fifty: storage private endpoints are per service. Blob, Data Lake, Files, queues, and tables each need their own private endpoint, and their own privatelink DNS zone. A private endpoint for blob does not cover Azure Files. If you must allow public access, use network rules with a default deny, plus trusted Azure services.' },
    { sec: 'Storage deep dive', short: 'Data protection', title: 'Soft delete, versioning and immutability', items: [['refresh', 'Blob versioning + change feed', 'Every overwrite kept'], ['trash', 'Soft delete', 'Blobs and containers, 7 to 365 days'], ['clock', 'Point-in-time restore', 'Roll a container back'], ['lock', 'Immutable storage (WORM)', 'Time-based retention, legal hold'], ['shield', 'Resource lock', 'CanNotDelete on the account']],
      cmd: ['$ az storage account blob-service-properties update -g rg-shop -n stshopprod8842 \\', '    --enable-versioning true --enable-change-feed true \\', '    --enable-delete-retention true --delete-retention-days 30 \\', '    --enable-container-delete-retention true --container-delete-retention-days 30'],
      n: 'Step fifty one: protecting data. Turn on versioning and the change feed, so every overwrite is kept, and soft delete for blobs and containers, here thirty days. Point in time restore can roll a whole container back. Immutable storage with time based retention or legal hold makes data write once, read many, which protects backups from ransomware. And a delete lock protects the account itself.' },
    { sec: 'Storage deep dive', short: 'SAS done right', title: 'SAS tokens done right', cols: ['Type', 'Signed with', 'Use'],
      rows: [['User delegation SAS', 'Entra ID credentials', 'The safe choice: revocable, auditable'], ['Service / account SAS', 'Account key', 'Avoid: disable shared keys'], ['Stored access policy', 'Server-side policy', 'Revoke many SAS at once'], ['SAS expiration policy', 'Account setting', 'Alert on long-lived tokens']],
      tip: 'Keep SAS lifetimes short (minutes to hours) and scope them to one blob or container.',
      n: 'Step fifty two: shared access signatures. Prefer a user delegation SAS, signed with Entra ID credentials, which you can revoke and audit. Avoid account key SAS, which you can block entirely by disabling shared key access. Set a SAS expiration policy, so long lived tokens raise alerts. And keep tokens short lived, scoped to one blob or container.' },
    { sec: 'Storage deep dive', short: 'Lifecycle', title: 'Lifecycle management rules',
      cmd: ['{ "rules": [{ "name": "logs", "enabled": true, "type": "Lifecycle",', '  "definition": { "filters": { "blobTypes": ["blockBlob"], "prefixMatch": ["logs/"] },', '    "actions": { "baseBlob": {', '      "tierToCool":    { "daysAfterModificationGreaterThan": 30 },', '      "tierToCold":    { "daysAfterModificationGreaterThan": 90 },', '      "tierToArchive": { "daysAfterModificationGreaterThan": 180 },', '      "delete":        { "daysAfterModificationGreaterThan": 2555 } },', '    "version": { "delete": { "daysAfterCreationGreaterThan": 30 } } } } }] }'],
      tip: 'Archive needs hours to rehydrate. Cool, cold and archive have minimum retention charges.',
      n: 'Step fifty three: lifecycle rules. Logs move to cool after thirty days, cold after ninety, archive after a hundred and eighty, and are deleted after seven years. Old versions are deleted after thirty days, or versioning quietly doubles your bill. Remember that archive data needs hours to rehydrate, and cool, cold, and archive charge early deletion fees.' },
    { sec: 'Storage deep dive', short: 'Redundancy choice', title: 'Choose redundancy', cols: ['SKU', 'Copies', 'Survives'],
      rows: [['LRS', '3 in one datacenter', 'Disk and rack failure'], ['ZRS', '3 across availability zones', 'A zone outage'], ['GRS / RA-GRS', 'LRS + 3 in paired region', 'A region outage (read with RA)'], ['GZRS / RA-GZRS', 'ZRS + 3 in paired region', 'Zone and region outages']],
      n: 'Step fifty four: redundancy. Locally redundant keeps three copies in one datacentre. Zone redundant spreads them across availability zones, the right default for production. Geo redundant adds three copies in the paired region, and read access versions let you read from there at any time. G Z R S combines zones and a second region, for the most critical data.' },
    { sec: 'Storage deep dive', short: 'Defender & keys', title: 'Customer-managed keys and Defender for Storage',
      items: [['key', 'Customer-managed keys', 'Key Vault or Managed HSM, auto-rotate'], ['lock', 'Infrastructure encryption', 'Double encryption at rest'], ['search', 'Defender for Storage', 'Malware scanning on upload'], ['eye', 'Sensitive data threat detection', 'Alerts on unusual access to PII']],
      n: 'Step fifty five: keys and threat protection. Data is always encrypted at rest; customer managed keys in Key Vault or Managed HSM let you control and rotate the key yourself, and infrastructure encryption adds a second layer. Defender for Storage scans uploads for malware, and raises alerts on unusual access, especially to containers holding sensitive data.' },

    { sec: 'Databases', short: 'Azure SQL', title: 'Azure SQL Database',
      cmd: ['$ az sql server create -g rg-shop-dev -n sql-shop-dev --enable-ad-only-auth \\', '    --external-admin-principal-type Group --external-admin-name "sql-admins" --external-admin-sid $GROUP_ID', '$ az sql db create -g rg-shop-dev -s sql-shop-dev -n orders --edition GeneralPurpose \\', '    --compute-model Serverless --family Gen5 --capacity 2 --auto-pause-delay 60'],
      tip: 'Serverless tier auto-pauses when idle, which is ideal for dev databases.',
      n: 'Step fifty six: Azure SQL Database is managed SQL Server, with backups, patching, and high availability built in. Use Entra only authentication, so there are no SQL passwords to leak. The serverless tier scales automatically and pauses when idle, which is perfect for development. Point in time restore, geo replication, and automatic tuning are included, which saves a lot of database administration work.' },
    { sec: 'Databases', short: 'Open-source DBs', title: 'PostgreSQL and MySQL Flexible Server',
      cmd: ['$ az postgres flexible-server create -g rg-shop-dev -n pg-shop-dev --tier GeneralPurpose \\', '    --sku-name Standard_D2ds_v5 --version 16 --high-availability ZoneRedundant \\', '    --vnet vnet-shop --subnet snet-data', '$ az postgres flexible-server show -g rg-shop-dev -n pg-shop-dev --query state', '"Ready"'],
      n: 'Step fifty seven: open-source databases. Azure Database for PostgreSQL and MySQL Flexible Server are fully managed, with zone redundant high availability, automatic backups, and private networking through VNet integration. Choose version, size, and storage, and scale them later without rebuilding. Use the built-in high availability, and connect apps with managed identities instead of database passwords where the engine supports it.' },
    { sec: 'Databases', short: 'Cosmos DB & cache', title: 'Cosmos DB and Azure Cache for Redis',
      items: [['globe', 'Cosmos DB', 'Global, multi-model NoSQL'], ['layers', 'APIs: NoSQL, MongoDB, Cassandra…', 'Pick per workload'], ['gauge', 'Request units (RU/s)', 'Provisioned, autoscale or serverless'], ['zap', 'Azure Cache for Redis', 'Sessions and hot data']],
      n: 'Step fifty eight: Cosmos DB is a globally distributed NoSQL database with single digit millisecond latency, and APIs for NoSQL, MongoDB, Cassandra, and more. Throughput is measured in request units, which you provision, autoscale, or pay per use. And Azure Cache for Redis keeps sessions and hot data in memory.' },
    { sec: 'Databases deep dive', short: 'SQL options', title: 'Azure SQL: which deployment?', cols: ['Option', 'Best for', 'Note'],
      rows: [['SQL Database (vCore)', 'Most new apps', 'GP, Business Critical, Hyperscale'], ['Serverless', 'Spiky or dev workloads', 'Auto-pause, per-second billing'], ['Elastic pool', 'Many small databases', 'Share capacity across tenants'], ['SQL Managed Instance', 'Lift-and-shift from SQL Server', 'SQL Agent, cross-db queries']],
      tip: 'Hyperscale scales storage to 128 TB and adds fast named replicas.',
      n: 'Step fifty nine: Azure SQL choices. Azure SQL Database suits most new applications, in General Purpose, Business Critical, or Hyperscale tiers. Serverless pauses when idle and bills per second. Elastic pools share capacity across many small databases, typical for software as a service. And SQL Managed Instance is for moving existing SQL Server workloads that need agent jobs or cross database queries.' },
    { sec: 'Databases deep dive', short: 'Secure SQL', title: 'Secure Azure SQL, layer by layer',
      cmd: ['$ az sql server create -g rg-shop -n sql-shop-prod -l westeurope --enable-ad-only-auth \\', '    --external-admin-principal-type Group --external-admin-name sql-admins \\', '    --external-admin-sid $GROUP_ID --minimal-tls-version 1.2 \\', '    --enable-public-network false --restrict-outbound-network-access true', '$ az sql db create -g rg-shop -s sql-shop-prod -n orders --edition GeneralPurpose \\', '    --compute-model Serverless --family Gen5 --capacity 2 --zone-redundant true --ledger-on Enabled'],
      tip: 'Entra-only auth removes SQL passwords. Restrict outbound blocks data exfiltration from the server.',
      n: 'Step sixty: securing Azure SQL. Entra ID only authentication removes SQL passwords entirely, with a group as admin. TLS one point two minimum, public network access disabled, and a private endpoint. Restrict outbound network access stops the server sending data to unexpected places. The database is zone redundant, serverless, and has ledger enabled, which makes tampering with history detectable.' },
    { sec: 'Databases deep dive', short: 'SQL protection', title: 'Auditing, Defender, TDE and Always Encrypted', items: [['eye', 'Auditing', 'To Log Analytics or storage'], ['search', 'Defender for SQL', 'SQL injection, brute force, anomalies'], ['lock', 'TDE with customer key', 'Encryption at rest'], ['key', 'Always Encrypted', 'Columns encrypted in the client'], ['users', 'Row-level security + masking', 'Users see only their rows']],
      n: 'Step sixty one: more database protection. Auditing records every query to Log Analytics. Defender for SQL detects SQL injection, brute force attempts, and unusual access. Transparent data encryption, with your own key, protects data at rest. Always Encrypted protects sensitive columns so even database admins cannot read them. And row level security with dynamic masking limits what each user sees.' },
    { sec: 'Databases deep dive', short: 'SQL HA/DR', title: 'High availability and geo-DR for SQL', cols: ['Feature', 'Protects against', 'RPO / RTO'],
      rows: [['Zone redundancy', 'A zone outage', 'Zero / seconds'], ['Point-in-time restore', 'Mistakes, up to 35 days', 'Minutes / minutes to hours'], ['Active geo-replication', 'Region outage, readable copy', 'Seconds / under a minute'], ['Failover groups', 'Region outage, one endpoint', 'Seconds / automatic or manual']],
      tip: 'Failover groups keep the same listener name, so apps reconnect without config changes.',
      n: 'Step sixty two: availability and disaster recovery. Zone redundancy survives a zone outage with no data loss. Point in time restore recovers from mistakes, for up to thirty five days, and long term retention keeps backups for years. Active geo replication keeps readable copies in other regions. Failover groups add one listener name that follows the primary, so applications reconnect without configuration changes.' },
    { sec: 'Databases deep dive', short: 'PostgreSQL Flexible', title: 'PostgreSQL Flexible Server in depth',
      cmd: ['$ az postgres flexible-server create -g rg-shop -n pg-shop-prod --version 17 \\', '    --tier GeneralPurpose --sku-name Standard_D4ds_v5 --high-availability ZoneRedundant \\', '    --vnet vnet-shop --subnet snet-pg --private-dns-zone pg-shop.private.postgres.database.azure.com \\', '    --microsoft-entra-auth Enabled --password-auth Disabled --backup-retention 35 --geo-redundant-backup Enabled', '$ az postgres flexible-server replica create -g rg-dr -n pg-shop-dr --source-server $PG_ID --location northeurope'],
      tip: 'Use the built-in PgBouncer, enable Entra auth, and disable passwords once apps use managed identity.',
      n: 'Step sixty three: PostgreSQL Flexible Server. Zone redundant high availability keeps a standby in another zone. VNet integration places it in a delegated subnet with a private DNS zone. Entra authentication lets apps log in with managed identities, and once they do, disable password authentication. Thirty five days of geo redundant backups, plus a read replica in another region that you can promote during a disaster.' },
    { sec: 'Databases deep dive', short: 'Cosmos DB', title: 'Cosmos DB: partitioning and consistency', cols: ['Consistency level', 'Guarantee', 'Cost'],
      rows: [['Strong', 'Always the latest write', 'Highest latency'], ['Bounded staleness', 'At most K versions or T time behind', 'High'], ['Session (default)', 'Read your own writes', 'Balanced'], ['Eventual', 'Converges over time', 'Lowest']],
      tip: 'Choose a high-cardinality partition key like /customerId. A bad key creates hot partitions forever.',
      n: 'Step sixty four: Cosmos DB. The partition key decides how data spreads, so choose a high cardinality key, like customer I D, and query by it. Five consistency levels trade freshness for latency and cost; session is the default and fits most apps, because users always read their own writes. Multi region writes give active active, and disabling local auth plus private endpoints secures it.' },
    { sec: 'Databases deep dive', short: 'Managed Redis', title: 'Azure Managed Redis', items: [['zap', 'Azure Managed Redis', 'Successor to Azure Cache for Redis'], ['layers', 'Active geo-replication', 'Multi-region caches'], ['key', 'Entra ID authentication', 'No access keys'], ['lock', 'Private endpoint only', 'No public access']],
      tip: 'Azure Cache for Redis is being retired. Plan migrations to Azure Managed Redis.',
      n: 'Step sixty five: caching. Azure Managed Redis is the successor to Azure Cache for Redis, which is being retired, so new projects should start there. It supports active geo replication across regions, Entra ID authentication instead of access keys, and private endpoints. Cache aside is the usual pattern: read the cache first, then the database, and write the result back with a time to live.' },

    { sec: 'Containers', short: 'ACR', title: 'Azure Container Registry',
      cmd: ['$ az acr create -g rg-shop-dev -n acrshop8842 --sku Standard', '$ az acr login -n acrshop8842', '$ docker push acrshop8842.azurecr.io/shop/api:1.4.2', '$ az acr build -r acrshop8842 -t shop/api:1.4.3 .', 'Run ID: ca1 was successful after 52s'],
      tip: 'az acr build builds in the cloud, so no local Docker is needed.',
      n: 'Step sixty six: Azure Container Registry stores your images privately. Log in with az acr login, then push. Even simpler, az acr build sends your source and Dockerfile to Azure, and builds the image in the cloud, with no local Docker needed. Premium adds geo replication. Turn on Defender for container registries to scan images for vulnerabilities, and add retention policies for untagged manifests.' },
    { sec: 'Containers', short: 'AKS', title: 'Kubernetes on Azure: AKS',
      cmd: ['$ az aks create -g rg-shop-dev -n aks-shop --node-count 3 --zones 1 2 3 \\', '    --network-plugin azure --network-plugin-mode overlay --enable-managed-identity \\', '    --enable-oidc-issuer --enable-workload-identity --attach-acr acrshop8842 --generate-ssh-keys', '$ az aks get-credentials -g rg-shop-dev -n aks-shop', '$ kubectl get nodes', 'aks-nodepool1-31718369-vmss000000   Ready   agent   3m   v1.37.2'],
      tip: 'The control plane is free on the Free tier; Standard tier adds an SLA.',
      n: 'Step sixty seven: AKS is managed Kubernetes. One command creates a cluster across three zones, with Azure CNI overlay networking, a managed identity, workload identity, and pull access to your registry. get credentials connects kubectl. Choose the Standard tier for a financially backed uptime SLA. Plan regular upgrades: Kubernetes versions are supported for about a year, and AKS can upgrade the control plane and node pools for you on a schedule.' },
    { sec: 'Containers', short: 'AKS add-ons', title: 'AKS essentials and add-ons',
      items: [['key', 'Workload Identity', 'Pods get Entra tokens, no secrets'], ['scale', 'Cluster autoscaler · Node auto-provisioning', 'Right-size nodes'], ['route', 'App routing · Application Gateway for Containers', 'Ingress options'], ['chart', 'Container Insights + Managed Prometheus', 'Monitoring']],
      n: 'Step sixty eight: AKS essentials. Workload identity lets pods get Entra tokens through a federated service account, with no secrets. The cluster autoscaler and node auto provisioning right-size your nodes. The app routing add-on and Application Gateway for Containers handle ingress. And Container Insights with managed Prometheus monitor it all.' },
    { sec: 'AKS deep dive', short: 'AKS Automatic', title: 'AKS Automatic: production defaults',
      cmd: ['$ az aks create -g rg-shop -n aks-shop --sku automatic', '$ az aks get-credentials -g rg-shop -n aks-shop', '$ kubectl get nodes', 'NAME                          STATUS   ROLES    AGE   VERSION', 'aks-default-7xkqz-ab12c       Ready    <none>   3m    v1.36.2'],
      tip: 'Automatic includes node autoprovisioning, Azure RBAC, workload identity, safeguards and managed Prometheus.',
      n: 'Step sixty nine: AKS Automatic. One command, az aks create with sku automatic, gives a production ready cluster: managed system node pools hosted by AKS, node autoprovisioning, Azure RBAC for Kubernetes, workload identity, API server VNet integration, deployment safeguards, and managed Prometheus and Container Insights. It comes with a pod readiness SLA: ninety nine point nine percent of pods ready within five minutes.' },
    { sec: 'AKS deep dive', short: 'Automatic vs Standard', title: 'AKS Automatic vs Standard', cols: ['', 'Automatic', 'Standard'],
      rows: [['Nodes', 'Node autoprovisioning (Karpenter)', 'You manage node pools'], ['Upgrades', 'Automatic, stable channel', 'Manual, or a channel you pick'], ['Security', 'Safeguards + PSS baseline enforced', 'Optional'], ['Auth', 'Azure RBAC, no local accounts', 'Local accounts by default'], ['Flexibility', 'Opinionated', 'Every knob available']],
      tip: 'Base clusters can now be converted to Automatic (preview, Oct 2026).',
      n: 'Step seventy: Automatic versus Standard. Automatic provisions nodes for you with node autoprovisioning, which is built on Karpenter, upgrades on the stable channel, enforces deployment safeguards and the baseline pod security standard, and uses Azure RBAC with no local accounts. Standard exposes every setting, but you own those choices. Existing clusters can now be converted to Automatic in preview.' },
    { sec: 'AKS deep dive', short: 'Secure Standard cluster', title: 'Create a secure AKS Standard cluster',
      cmd: ['$ az aks create -g rg-shop -n aks-shop --tier standard --kubernetes-version 1.37 \\', '    --network-plugin azure --network-plugin-mode overlay --network-dataplane cilium \\', '    --enable-private-cluster --enable-apiserver-vnet-integration \\', '    --enable-aad --enable-azure-rbac --disable-local-accounts \\', '    --enable-oidc-issuer --enable-workload-identity --enable-image-cleaner \\', '    --outbound-type userDefinedRouting --node-provisioning-mode Auto \\', '    --auto-upgrade-channel stable --node-os-upgrade-channel NodeImage --zones 1 2 3'],
      tip: 'Kubernetes 1.37 is GA on AKS (Oct 2026) and designated a Long-Term Support release.',
      n: 'Step seventy one: a secure Standard cluster. Azure CNI overlay with the Cilium data plane. A private API server with VNet integration. Entra ID with Azure RBAC, and local accounts disabled. The OIDC issuer and workload identity for pods. Outbound through the hub firewall with user defined routing. Node autoprovisioning, automatic upgrades, and three zones. Kubernetes one point three seven became generally available in October, as a long term support release.' },
    { sec: 'AKS deep dive', short: 'Networking', title: 'AKS networking models', cols: ['Model', 'Pod IPs', 'Choose when'],
      rows: [['Azure CNI Overlay', 'Private overlay range', 'Default: saves VNet addresses'], ['Azure CNI (pod subnet)', 'Real VNet IPs', 'Pods reachable directly from on-prem'], ['Cilium dataplane', 'eBPF, any of the above', 'Fast policy, Hubble visibility'], ['Advanced networking', 'Flow logs, FQDN and L7 policy', 'Zero-trust pod traffic']],
      tip: 'Kubenet is retired. Move to Azure CNI Overlay.',
      n: 'Step seventy two: AKS networking. Azure CNI overlay gives pods addresses from a private overlay range, saving VNet space, and is the default. Azure CNI with a pod subnet gives pods real VNet addresses, reachable from on premises. The Cilium data plane uses e B P F for fast network policy and Hubble visibility. Advanced Container Networking Services add flow logs, domain name policies, and layer seven policies. Kubenet is retired.' },
    { sec: 'AKS deep dive', short: 'Workload identity', title: 'Workload identity: pods to Key Vault with no secrets',
      cmd: ['$ az identity federated-credential create -g rg-shop --identity-name id-orders -n aks-orders \\', '    --issuer $(az aks show -g rg-shop -n aks-shop --query oidcIssuerProfile.issuerUrl -o tsv) \\', '    --subject system:serviceaccount:shop:orders --audiences api://AzureADTokenExchange', 'apiVersion: v1', 'kind: ServiceAccount', 'metadata: { name: orders, namespace: shop,', '  annotations: { azure.workload.identity/client-id: "<id-orders client id>" } }'],
      tip: 'Label pods azure.workload.identity/use: "true". The SDK picks up the token automatically.',
      n: 'Step seventy three: workload identity. Create a federated credential on a managed identity, trusting the cluster OIDC issuer, for exactly one service account: orders in the shop namespace. Annotate the service account with the identity client I D, and label the pods. The Azure SDK then exchanges the Kubernetes token for an Entra token, so the pod reads Key Vault with no secret anywhere.' },
    { sec: 'AKS deep dive', short: 'Ingress', title: 'Ingress on AKS', cols: ['Option', 'Implements', 'Note'],
      rows: [['App routing add-on', 'Managed NGINX + Gateway API', 'DNS and Key Vault certs built in'], ['AGC (containers)', 'Gateway API, managed L7', 'Traffic splitting, WAF'], ['Istio add-on gateway', 'Gateway API', 'With the service mesh'], ['Managed Gateway API', 'Gateway API v1.6 on 1.37', 'Installed and upgraded by AKS']],
      tip: 'Community ingress-nginx was archived in March 2026. AKS App Routing keeps a supported managed option.',
      n: 'Step seventy four: ingress. The application routing add on manages NGINX and Gateway API, with DNS records and Key Vault certificates built in. Application Gateway for Containers is a managed layer seven gateway with traffic splitting and WAF. The Istio add on has its own gateway. AKS can also install the Gateway API definitions for you, version one point six on Kubernetes one point three seven.' },
    { sec: 'AKS deep dive', short: 'Policy & safeguards', title: 'Deployment safeguards and Azure Policy for AKS', items: [['shield', 'Deployment safeguards', 'Warn or enforce best practices'], ['lock', 'Pod Security Standards', 'Baseline or restricted'], ['search', 'Image integrity', 'Only signed images (Notation)'], ['filter', 'Allowed registries', 'Only your ACR']],
      n: 'Step seventy five: guardrails inside the cluster. Deployment safeguards check every manifest against best practices, like resource limits and probes, in warning or enforcement mode. Pod security standards block privileged pods. Image integrity, using Notation signatures, admits only signed images. And an Azure Policy assignment limits images to your own container registry.' },
    { sec: 'AKS deep dive', short: 'Upgrades', title: 'Upgrade channels and maintenance windows', cols: ['Channel', 'Upgrades to'],
      rows: [['patch', 'Latest patch of your minor version'], ['stable', 'Latest patch of minor N-1'], ['rapid', 'Latest supported minor'], ['NodeImage', 'Weekly node OS image with security fixes']],
      tip: 'From June 2027 AKS force-upgrades clusters left on unsupported versions. Stay on a channel.',
      n: 'Step seventy six: upgrades. The patch channel keeps your minor version patched. Stable moves to the latest patch of the previous minor version, which is what Automatic uses. Rapid moves to the newest minor. The node image channel applies weekly security fixes to nodes. Pair them with planned maintenance windows. Starting June twenty twenty seven, AKS will force upgrade clusters left on unsupported versions, so stay on a channel.' },
    { sec: 'AKS deep dive', short: 'Observe AKS', title: 'Observability and fleets', items: [['chart', 'Managed Prometheus + Grafana', 'Metrics and dashboards'], ['list', 'Container Insights', 'Logs and live view'], ['eye', 'Cluster Health Monitor', 'Node checks and remediation'], ['layers', 'Azure Kubernetes Fleet Manager', 'Update and place across many clusters']],
      n: 'Step seventy seven: observing AKS. Managed Prometheus and Azure Managed Grafana for metrics and dashboards, Container Insights for logs, and the new Cluster Health Monitor checks nodes and repairs them. With many clusters, Azure Kubernetes Fleet Manager orchestrates upgrades in stages across clusters, and places workloads across them from one hub.' },
    { sec: 'AKS deep dive', short: 'Container Apps', title: 'Container Apps in depth', items: [['zap', 'Scale to zero with KEDA', 'HTTP, queues, events'], ['refresh', 'Revisions and traffic splitting', 'Blue-green and canary'], ['lock', 'Internal environments', 'VNet-injected, private'], ['clock', 'Jobs', 'Scheduled and event-driven tasks']],
      n: 'Step seventy eight: Container Apps in depth. Apps scale to zero, and back up, with KEDA rules for HTTP traffic, queues, or events. Every change creates a revision, and you can split traffic between revisions for blue green or canary releases. Internal environments inject into your VNet with no public endpoint. And jobs run scheduled or event driven tasks to completion.' },
    { sec: 'Containers', short: 'Container Apps', title: 'Azure Container Apps',
      cmd: ['$ az containerapp env create -g rg-shop-dev -n cae-shop --location westeurope', '$ az containerapp create -g rg-shop-dev -n api --environment cae-shop \\', '    --image acrshop8842.azurecr.io/shop/api:1.4.2 --target-port 8080 --ingress external \\', '    --min-replicas 0 --max-replicas 10 --registry-server acrshop8842.azurecr.io', 'https://api.purplebeach-1a2b3c4d.westeurope.azurecontainerapps.io'],
      tip: 'Kubernetes power without managing a cluster: KEDA scaling, Dapr and revisions built in.',
      n: 'Step seventy nine: Azure Container Apps runs containers without managing a cluster. It is built on Kubernetes, KEDA, and Envoy, so it scales on HTTP traffic or events, all the way to zero. Revisions enable traffic splitting, and Dapr is built in. It is a great middle ground between App Service and AKS.' },

    { sec: 'App platforms', short: 'App Service', title: 'App Service: managed web apps',
      cmd: ['$ az appservice plan create -g rg-shop-dev -n plan-shop --sku P0v3 --is-linux', '$ az webapp create -g rg-shop-dev -p plan-shop -n app-shop-8842 --runtime "NODE:24-lts"', '$ az webapp deploy -g rg-shop-dev -n app-shop-8842 --src-path app.zip', '$ az webapp deployment slot create -g rg-shop-dev -n app-shop-8842 --slot staging'],
      tip: 'Deploy to a staging slot, warm it up, then swap with zero downtime.',
      n: 'Step eighty: App Service hosts web apps and APIs without managing servers. Create a plan, which sets the size and price, then a web app with a runtime like Node or .NET, and deploy a zip or a container. Deployment slots let you deploy to staging, warm it up, and swap into production with zero downtime. Built-in authentication, custom domains with free managed certificates, and VNet integration make it a fast path to production.' },
    { sec: 'App platforms', short: 'App Service secure', title: 'App Service, secured and highly available', items: [['refresh', 'Deployment slots', 'Swap staging to production, instant rollback'], ['link', 'VNet integration + private endpoint', 'Private in and out'], ['key', 'Managed identity + Key Vault references', 'No secrets in app settings'], ['layers', 'Zone redundancy (Premium v3/v4)', 'Survive a zone outage']],
      n: 'Step eighty one: App Service, secured. Deployment slots let you deploy to staging, warm it up, and swap into production, with instant swap back. VNet integration sends outbound traffic into your network, and a private endpoint makes inbound private. App settings reference Key Vault secrets through a managed identity. And zone redundant premium plans survive a zone outage.' },
    { sec: 'App platforms', short: 'Choose a platform', title: 'Choose the right compute', cols: ['You manage', 'Best for'],
      rows: [['Virtual Machines', 'OS and everything above', 'Lift-and-shift, full control'], ['App Service', 'Code or container', 'Web apps and APIs'], ['Container Apps', 'Containers', 'Microservices, event-driven'], ['AKS', 'Kubernetes workloads', 'Complex platforms'], ['Functions', 'Just functions', 'Events and glue code']],
      n: 'Step eighty two: choose the right compute. Virtual machines give full control. App Service runs web apps from code or containers. Container Apps runs microservices with event-driven scaling. AKS gives full Kubernetes. And Functions runs small pieces of code on events. Start as high up this list as your app allows.' },

    { sec: 'Serverless', short: 'Azure Functions', title: 'Azure Functions',
      cmd: ['$ func init shop-fn --python && cd shop-fn', '$ func new --name process-order --template "Azure Queue Storage trigger"', '$ az functionapp create -g rg-shop-dev -n fn-shop-8842 --storage-account stshopdev8842 \\', '    --flexconsumption-location westeurope --runtime python --runtime-version 3.11', '$ func azure functionapp publish fn-shop-8842'],
      tip: 'Flex Consumption scales fast, supports VNets, and bills per execution.',
      n: 'Step eighty three: Azure Functions runs code in response to events: HTTP requests, timers, queue messages, or blob uploads. The Functions core tools create a project locally, and publish deploys it. The Flex Consumption plan scales quickly, supports private networking, and bills per execution. Durable Functions add long-running, stateful workflows with checkpoints, written as ordinary code.' },
    { sec: 'Serverless', short: 'Functions plans', title: 'Functions hosting plans', cols: ['Plan', 'Scale', 'Best for'],
      rows: [['Flex Consumption', 'Fast, per-instance concurrency, VNet', 'Most new event-driven apps'], ['Premium', 'Pre-warmed, no cold start', 'Steady low latency'], ['Dedicated plan', 'Your plan, your instances', 'Predictable cost'], ['Container Apps', 'KEDA scaling, containers', 'Functions as containers']],
      tip: 'Flex Consumption replaces the Linux Consumption plan for new apps, and supports VNets.',
      n: 'Step eighty four: where Functions run. Flex Consumption scales fast, bills per use, supports virtual networks, and is the recommended plan for new event driven apps. Premium keeps instances pre warmed, removing cold starts. A dedicated App Service plan gives predictable cost. And Functions can also run on Container Apps, scaled by KEDA. Durable Functions add stateful workflows in code.' },
    { sec: 'Serverless', short: 'Messaging', title: 'Messaging: Service Bus, Event Grid, Event Hubs', cols: ['Pattern', 'Use it for'],
      rows: [['Service Bus', 'Queues and topics, transactions', 'Reliable business messages'], ['Event Grid', 'Push events, filters', 'React to Azure and app events'], ['Event Hubs', 'High-throughput streaming', 'Telemetry, Kafka-compatible'], ['Storage Queues', 'Simple, cheap queues', 'Basic background jobs']],
      tip: 'Use dead-letter queues and idempotent consumers.',
      n: 'Step eighty five: messaging. Service Bus provides reliable queues and topics for business messages, with ordering and transactions. Event Grid pushes events, like a blob being created, to subscribers. Event Hubs ingests millions of events per second, and speaks the Kafka protocol. Always plan for dead letters and duplicate messages.' },
    { sec: 'Serverless', short: 'Logic Apps & APIM', title: 'Logic Apps and API Management',
      items: [['pipeline', 'Logic Apps', 'Low-code workflows, 1,000+ connectors'], ['route', 'API Management', 'Gateway, policies, developer portal'], ['key', 'APIM policies', 'Auth, rate limits, transforms'], ['link', 'Connect SaaS and on-prem', 'Office 365, SAP, Salesforce…']],
      n: 'Step eighty six: integration. Logic Apps builds workflows with a visual designer and over a thousand connectors, including Office three six five, SAP, and Salesforce. API Management publishes APIs behind a gateway, with policies for authentication, rate limiting, and transformation, plus a developer portal.' },
    { sec: 'Serverless', short: 'Messaging deep', title: 'Service Bus vs Event Grid vs Event Hubs', cols: ['Service', 'Model', 'Use for'],
      rows: [['Service Bus', 'Queues, topics, sessions', 'Business commands: orders, payments'], ['Event Grid', 'Push events, filtering, MQTT', 'React to resource and app events'], ['Event Hubs', 'Partitioned stream, Kafka API', 'Telemetry and logs, millions per second'], ['Storage queues', 'Simple queue', 'Basic background work']],
      tip: 'Use dead-lettering and sessions in Service Bus. Disable local auth and use managed identities.',
      n: 'Step eighty seven: choosing messaging. Service Bus is for business commands like orders and payments: queues and topics with ordering through sessions, transactions, and dead lettering. Event Grid pushes events, with filtering, when something happens. Event Hubs ingests partitioned streams, millions of events per second, and speaks the Kafka protocol. Disable local auth on all of them.' },
    { sec: 'Serverless', short: 'APIM secure', title: 'API Management as a secure front door for APIs', items: [['key', 'validate-jwt policy', 'Check Entra ID tokens'], ['gauge', 'rate-limit-by-key', 'Per subscription or caller'], ['lock', 'Internal VNet mode', 'Private APIs behind App Gateway'], ['share', 'Self-hosted gateway', 'Same policies on-prem or in AKS']],
      n: 'Step eighty eight: API Management. Policies run on every call: validate JWT checks Entra ID tokens, rate limit by key throttles each caller, and backends authenticate with managed identity. Internal VNet mode keeps APIs private, published through Application Gateway with WAF. A self hosted gateway applies the same policies in AKS or on premises.' },

    { sec: 'Infrastructure as code', short: 'ARM & Bicep', title: 'Infrastructure as code with Bicep',
      cmd: ["param location string = resourceGroup().location", "resource sa 'Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts@2023-05-01' = {", "  name: 'stshop${uniqueString(resourceGroup().id)}'", '  location: location', "  sku: { name: 'Standard_ZRS' }", "  kind: 'StorageV2'", '}', '$ az deployment group what-if -g rg-shop-dev -f main.bicep', '$ az deployment group create -g rg-shop-dev -f main.bicep'],
      tip: 'what-if previews every change before you deploy.',
      n: 'Step eighty nine: infrastructure as code. Bicep is Azure\'s native language, a much cleaner way to write ARM templates. Declare resources with parameters, preview changes with what if, then deploy to a resource group. Azure Verified Modules provide tested, reusable building blocks.' },
    { sec: 'Infrastructure as code', short: 'Terraform on Azure', title: 'Terraform and deployment stacks', cols: ['Language', 'Strength'],
      rows: [['Bicep', 'Bicep (compiles to ARM)', 'Native, day-zero support'], ['Terraform / OpenTofu', 'HCL, azurerm provider', 'Multi-cloud, huge ecosystem'], ['Deployment stacks', 'Bicep / ARM', 'Manage and clean up as a unit'], ['Pulumi', 'TypeScript, Python, Go, C#', 'General-purpose languages']],
      n: 'Step ninety: other options. Terraform, with the azurerm provider, is popular for multi-cloud teams; store its state in a storage account with locking. Deployment stacks manage a set of Bicep resources as a unit, including clean up. And Pulumi uses general-purpose languages. Whichever you choose, run it from a pipeline, review the plan or what if output, and never deploy production changes from a laptop.' },
    { sec: 'Infrastructure as code', short: 'Bicep modules', title: 'Bicep modules and Azure Verified Modules',
      cmd: ['module vnet \'br/public:avm/res/network/virtual-network:0.7.0\' = {', '  params: {', '    name: \'vnet-shop\'', '    addressPrefixes: [ \'10.10.0.0/16\' ]', '    subnets: [ { name: \'snet-app\', addressPrefix: \'10.10.1.0/24\' } ]', '  }', '}', '$ az deployment group what-if -g rg-shop -f main.bicep'],
      tip: 'Azure Verified Modules exist for Bicep and Terraform. what-if previews changes like a plan.',
      n: 'Step ninety one: modules. Azure Verified Modules are Microsoft maintained building blocks, for Bicep and Terraform, with secure defaults and tests. Reference one from the public registry, pass parameters, and you get a well configured virtual network. Always run what if before deploying: it shows every resource that will be created, changed, or deleted, like a Terraform plan.' },
    { sec: 'Infrastructure as code', short: 'Deployment stacks', title: 'Deployment stacks: manage and protect a whole set',
      cmd: ['$ az stack sub create --name shop-platform --location westeurope \\', '    --template-file platform.bicep --action-on-unmanage deleteResources \\', '    --deny-settings-mode denyWriteAndDelete --deny-settings-excluded-principals $PIPELINE_SP'],
      tip: 'Deny settings stop anyone except the pipeline from changing stack resources in the portal.',
      n: 'Step ninety two: deployment stacks manage a whole group of resources as one unit. Remove a resource from the template, and action on unmanage deletes it, so nothing is orphaned. Deny settings block writes and deletes from anyone except excluded principals, like the pipeline, which stops manual portal changes from drifting your infrastructure.' },
    { sec: 'Infrastructure as code', short: 'azd', title: 'Azure Developer CLI (azd)',
      cmd: ['$ azd init --template todo-nodejs-mongo', '$ azd up', '(✓) Done: Resource group: rg-todo-dev', '(✓) Done: Container App: ca-api-7xk2', 'Deploying services (azd deploy)', '  Endpoint: https://ca-web-7xk2.westeurope.azurecontainerapps.io/'],
      tip: 'azd up = provision infrastructure + build + deploy, from a template.',
      n: 'Step ninety three: the Azure Developer CLI, called a z d, takes an app template, with code, Bicep, and pipeline files, and runs azd up to provision infrastructure, build, and deploy in one command. It is a fast way to learn good project structure.' },

    { sec: 'CI/CD', short: 'Azure Pipelines', title: 'CI/CD with Azure Pipelines',
      nodes: [['git', 'Repo', 'GitHub / Azure Repos'], ['box', 'Build stage', 'test + image'], ['database', 'ACR'], ['rocket', 'Deploy stage', 'AKS / App Service']], edges: ['trigger', 'push', 'approval gate'],
      tip: 'Use an Azure Resource Manager service connection with workload identity federation.',
      n: 'Step ninety four: Azure Pipelines, part of Azure DevOps, builds, tests, and deploys with YAML pipelines. A build stage tests code and pushes an image to ACR. A deploy stage, protected by environment approvals, releases to AKS or App Service. Connect to Azure with a service connection that uses workload identity federation.' },
    { sec: 'CI/CD', short: 'GitHub Actions', title: 'CI/CD with GitHub Actions',
      cmd: ['permissions: { id-token: write, contents: read }', 'steps:', '  - uses: azure/login@v3', '    with: { client-id: ${{ vars.AZURE_CLIENT_ID }}, tenant-id: ${{ vars.AZURE_TENANT_ID }}, subscription-id: ${{ vars.AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID }} }', '  - run: az acr build -r acrshop8842 -t shop/api:${{ github.sha }} .', '  - run: az containerapp update -g rg-shop-dev -n api --image acrshop8842.azurecr.io/shop/api:${{ github.sha }}'],
      n: 'Step ninety five: GitHub Actions works just as well. azure login uses OIDC with a client id, tenant, and subscription, and no secret. Then build the image in ACR, tagged with the commit, and update the container app. GitHub environments add approvals and protection rules. Both platforms can deploy with blue green or canary strategies, using App Service slots, Container Apps revisions, or Kubernetes rollouts.' },
    { sec: 'CI/CD', short: 'Pipeline security', title: 'Secure pipelines on Azure', items: [['key', 'Workload identity federation', 'No client secrets in service connections'], ['shield', 'Environments with approvals', 'Production needs a human'], ['lock', 'Managed DevOps Pools / private runners', 'Reach private endpoints'], ['search', 'Defender for DevOps', 'Secrets, IaC and dependency scanning']],
      n: 'Step ninety six: securing pipelines. Service connections and GitHub workflows should use workload identity federation, never client secrets. Production deployments go through environments with required approvals. Self hosted runners, or Managed DevOps Pools, run inside your network, so they can reach private endpoints. And Defender for DevOps scans repositories for secrets, infrastructure misconfigurations, and vulnerable dependencies.' },

    { sec: 'Observability', short: 'Azure Monitor', title: 'Azure Monitor: metrics and alerts',
      cmd: ['$ az monitor metrics alert create -g rg-shop-dev -n alert-api-5xx \\', '    --scopes $APP_ID --condition "total Http5xx > 50" --window-size 5m --evaluation-frequency 1m \\', '    --action $ACTION_GROUP_ID --severity 1', '$ az monitor metrics list --resource $APP_ID --metric Http5xx --interval PT1M -o table'],
      tip: 'Action groups decide who is notified: email, SMS, Teams, webhooks, runbooks.',
      n: 'Step ninety seven: Azure Monitor collects platform metrics from every resource automatically. A metric alert fires when server errors exceed fifty in five minutes, and an action group decides what happens: email, SMS, a Teams message, a webhook, or an automation runbook.' },
    { sec: 'Observability', short: 'Log Analytics & KQL', title: 'Logs with Log Analytics and KQL',
      cmd: ['AppRequests', '| where TimeGenerated > ago(1h) and Success == false', '| summarize failures = count() by OperationName, bin(TimeGenerated, 5m)', '| order by failures desc', '| take 10', '$ az monitor diagnostic-settings create --resource $APP_ID -n to-law --workspace $LAW_ID --logs \'[{"categoryGroup":"allLogs","enabled":true}]\''],
      tip: 'Turn on diagnostic settings, or resource logs never reach the workspace.',
      n: 'Step ninety eight: logs. A Log Analytics workspace stores logs, and you query them with KQL, the Kusto Query Language: filter, summarise by time bins, and sort. Remember to create diagnostic settings for each resource, because resource logs are not collected until you do. Set retention and table plans thoughtfully, because log ingestion is often one of the biggest monitoring costs.' },
    { sec: 'Observability', short: 'App Insights', title: 'Application Insights',
      items: [['search', 'Requests, dependencies, exceptions', 'Distributed tracing'], ['map', 'Application map', 'See every call between services'], ['globe', 'Availability tests', 'Probe from around the world'], ['code', 'OpenTelemetry distro', 'Instrument once, standard APIs']],
      n: 'Step ninety nine: Application Insights adds application performance monitoring. It traces requests, dependencies, and exceptions, draws an application map of calls between services, and runs availability tests from around the world. Instrument with the Azure Monitor OpenTelemetry distribution.' },
    { sec: 'Observability', short: 'KQL you need', title: 'KQL queries you will use every week',
      cmd: ['AzureDiagnostics', '| where Category == "AzureFirewallNetworkRule" and msg_s has "Deny"', '| summarize denies = count() by bin(TimeGenerated, 15m), SourceIP = extract(@"from (\\S+):", 1, msg_s)', '| top 10 by denies', 'SigninLogs', '| where ResultType != 0 | summarize fails = count() by UserPrincipalName, IPAddress | where fails > 20'],
      tip: 'Save queries as functions, and turn the important ones into alert rules.',
      n: 'Step one hundred: queries you will use every week. The first finds which source IPs the firewall is denying most, in fifteen minute buckets. The second finds accounts with more than twenty failed sign ins, a sign of password spraying. Save queries as functions, pin them to workbooks, and turn the important ones into log search alerts.' },
    { sec: 'Observability', short: 'Alerts & action groups', title: 'Alerts, action groups and SLOs', items: [['bell', 'Metric and log alerts', 'Dynamic thresholds learn normal'], ['users', 'Action groups', 'Email, SMS, Teams, webhook, runbook'], ['gauge', 'Availability tests', 'Probe endpoints from many regions'], ['check', 'Service health alerts', 'Know about Azure incidents first']],
      n: 'Step one hundred one: alerting. Metric and log alerts, with dynamic thresholds that learn what normal looks like, notify action groups: email, SMS, Teams, a webhook, or an automation runbook. Availability tests probe your endpoints from many regions. And service health alerts tell you when Azure itself has an incident or planned maintenance affecting your resources.' },

    { sec: 'Security & reliability', short: 'Key Vault', title: 'Secrets with Key Vault',
      cmd: ['$ az keyvault create -g rg-shop-dev -n kv-shop-8842 --enable-rbac-authorization true', '$ az keyvault secret set --vault-name kv-shop-8842 -n db-password --value "$(openssl rand -base64 24)"', '$ az role assignment create --assignee-object-id $API_IDENTITY --role "Key Vault Secrets User" \\', '    --scope $(az keyvault show -n kv-shop-8842 --query id -o tsv) --assignee-principal-type ServicePrincipal'],
      tip: 'Use RBAC mode, managed identities, and Key Vault references in App Service.',
      n: 'Step one hundred two: Key Vault stores secrets, keys, and certificates. Use RBAC authorisation, and grant each app\'s managed identity the Key Vault Secrets User role. App Service and Functions can read secrets through Key Vault references, so values never appear in settings or code. Turn on purge protection and soft delete, so a deleted vault or secret can always be recovered.' },
    { sec: 'Security & reliability', short: 'Defender & Sentinel', title: 'Microsoft Defender for Cloud and Sentinel',
      items: [['shield', 'Secure score + recommendations', 'Posture management'], ['eye', 'Defender plans', 'Servers, containers, storage, SQL'], ['search', 'Microsoft Sentinel', 'Cloud SIEM and SOAR'], ['lock', 'DDoS Protection · WAF', 'Protect public endpoints']],
      n: 'Step one hundred three: security operations. Microsoft Defender for Cloud scores your security posture, recommends fixes, and adds threat protection plans for servers, containers, storage, and databases. Microsoft Sentinel is a cloud SIEM that collects signals and automates response. And DDoS Protection with a WAF guards public endpoints.' },
    { sec: 'Security deep dive', short: 'Key Vault properly', title: 'Key Vault, configured properly',
      cmd: ['$ az keyvault create -g rg-shop -n kv-shop-prod-8842 --enable-rbac-authorization true \\', '    --enable-purge-protection true --retention-days 90 --public-network-access Disabled', '$ az role assignment create --assignee-object-id $ORDERS_MI \\', '    --role "Key Vault Secrets User" --scope $KV_ID/secrets/db-password', '$ az keyvault key rotation-policy update --vault-name kv-shop-prod-8842 -n cmk-sql --value @rotate-90d.json'],
      tip: 'Purge protection means a deleted vault cannot be destroyed for 90 days, even by an admin.',
      n: 'Step one hundred four: Key Vault, properly. Use the RBAC permission model, not access policies. Enable purge protection with ninety days of retention, so a deleted vault or key cannot be destroyed, even by an attacker with admin rights. Disable public access and use a private endpoint. Grant apps the secrets user role on a single secret, and set a rotation policy on keys.' },
    { sec: 'Security deep dive', short: 'Defender plans', title: 'Defender for Cloud plans', cols: ['Plan', 'Protects'],
      rows: [['Defender CSPM', 'Attack paths, cloud security explorer'], ['Defender for Servers', 'EDR, vulnerability assessment, JIT VM access'], ['Defender for Containers', 'Image scanning, runtime threats on AKS'], ['Defender for data', 'Malware, injection, unusual access'], ['DevOps security', 'Secrets and IaC issues in repos and pipelines']],
      tip: 'Attack path analysis shows how an internet-facing VM could reach your data. Fix those first.',
      n: 'Step one hundred five: Defender for Cloud plans. Defender CSPM finds attack paths, for example an internet facing VM with a vulnerability and a role that can read your database. Defender for Servers adds endpoint detection and just in time VM access. Defender for Containers scans images and watches AKS at runtime. There are plans for storage, SQL, and Key Vault, and DevOps security scans your repos and pipelines.' },
    { sec: 'Security deep dive', short: 'Sentinel', title: 'Microsoft Sentinel in practice', nodes: [['list', 'Data connectors', 'Entra, Azure Activity, Defender'], ['search', 'Analytics rules', 'KQL detections'], ['bell', 'Incidents', 'grouped alerts'], ['zap', 'Playbooks', 'Logic Apps automation']], edges: ['ingest', 'detect', 'respond'],
      tip: 'Start with the content hub solutions for Entra ID and Azure Activity, then tune false positives.',
      n: 'Step one hundred six: Microsoft Sentinel, the SIEM. Connect data: Entra sign ins, Azure activity, and Defender alerts. Analytics rules written in KQL detect threats, and group alerts into incidents. Playbooks, built on Logic Apps, respond automatically, for example disabling a compromised account. Start with the content hub solutions, then tune out the noise.' },
    { sec: 'Security deep dive', short: 'Logs everywhere', title: 'Diagnostic settings and Activity Log', items: [['list', 'Activity Log', 'Who changed what, 90 days by default'], ['gear', 'Diagnostic settings', 'Resource logs to Log Analytics'], ['shield', 'Policy: deploy if not exists', 'Every resource sends logs'], ['lock', 'Immutable archive', 'Storage with WORM for audits']],
      n: 'Step one hundred seven: logs everywhere. The Activity Log records every control plane change, who did what, but only for ninety days, so export it. Diagnostic settings send resource logs, like Key Vault access and firewall decisions, to Log Analytics. Use a deploy if not exists policy, so every new resource sends logs automatically. And archive to immutable storage for audits.' },
    { sec: 'Security deep dive', short: 'Incident response', title: 'When a credential leaks', items: [['lock', 'Revoke sessions', 'Entra: revoke refresh tokens, disable SP secret'], ['eye', 'Investigate', 'Sign-in logs, Activity Log, Sentinel'], ['search', 'Scope', 'New role assignments, new resources'], ['refresh', 'Recover', 'Rotate secrets, add Conditional Access']],
      n: 'Step one hundred eight: when a credential leaks. Contain it: revoke the user refresh tokens in Entra ID, or remove the service principal secret. Investigate sign in logs and the activity log in Sentinel. Look for new role assignments and new resources, especially in regions you never use. Then rotate every related secret, and add the Conditional Access rule or policy that would have stopped it.' },
    { sec: 'Security & reliability', short: 'Backup & DR', title: 'Backup and disaster recovery',
      items: [['database', 'Azure Backup', 'VMs, disks, SQL, files, AKS'], ['refresh', 'Azure Site Recovery', 'Replicate VMs to another region'], ['globe', 'Geo-replication', 'SQL, Cosmos DB, storage'], ['check', 'Test restores regularly', 'A backup is only real once restored']],
      n: 'Step one hundred nine: backup and recovery. Azure Backup protects VMs, disks, SQL, file shares, and AKS, with immutable vaults against ransomware. Site Recovery replicates VMs to another region for failover. Databases and storage offer geo replication. And test restores regularly, because an untested backup is only a hope.' },
    { sec: 'Reliability', short: 'Zones & regions', title: 'Availability zones and region pairs', items: [['layers', 'Zonal resources', 'Pinned to one zone: deploy 2 or 3'], ['refresh', 'Zone-redundant resources', 'Spread automatically: SQL, storage ZRS, AKS'], ['globe', 'Paired regions', 'Sequential updates, GRS target'], ['shield', 'Immutable backup vaults', 'Soft delete and multi-user authorization']],
      n: 'Step one hundred ten: designing for failure. Zonal resources, like a single VM, live in one zone, so deploy two or three. Zone redundant services, like Azure SQL, ZRS storage, and AKS, spread automatically. Paired regions receive platform updates one at a time, and are the target of geo redundant storage. Protect backups with immutable vaults, soft delete, and multi user authorization.' },
    { sec: 'Reliability', short: 'Chaos', title: 'Test resilience with Chaos Studio', items: [['bug', 'Faults', 'Shut down VMs, add latency, kill AKS pods'], ['clock', 'Experiments', 'Step by step, with stop conditions'], ['check', 'Load Testing', 'Run JMeter or Locust at scale'], ['refresh', 'Game days', 'Practise the failover runbook']],
      n: 'Step one hundred eleven: test resilience before reality does. Azure Chaos Studio runs experiments that shut down VMs, add network latency, or kill AKS pods, step by step, with stop conditions. Azure Load Testing runs JMeter or Locust tests at scale, often in pipelines. And regular game days make the failover runbook something the team has actually done.' },
    { sec: 'Security & reliability', short: 'Well-Architected', title: 'The Azure Well-Architected Framework',
      items: [['refresh', 'Reliability · Security'], ['dollar', 'Cost optimisation'], ['gear', 'Operational excellence'], ['zap', 'Performance efficiency', 'Plus Azure Advisor recommendations']],
      n: 'Step one hundred twelve: the Azure Well-Architected Framework has five pillars: reliability, security, cost optimisation, operational excellence, and performance efficiency. Azure Advisor gives personalised recommendations against each pillar, and the Well-Architected review assesses your workload.' },

    { sec: 'Cost', short: 'Cost Management', title: 'See where the money goes',
      cmd: ['$ az consumption budget create --budget-name shop-dev --amount 500 --time-grain Monthly \\', '    --start-date 2026-11-01 --end-date 2027-11-01 --category Cost --resource-group rg-shop-dev', '$ az costmanagement query --type ActualCost --timeframe MonthToDate \\', '    --scope /subscriptions/$SUB --dataset-grouping name=ResourceGroupName type=Dimension'],
      tip: 'Group costs by resource group and tags in Cost Management + Billing.',
      n: 'Step one hundred thirteen: cost visibility. Microsoft Cost Management shows spending by subscription, resource group, service, and tag. Create budgets with alerts at fifty, eighty, and one hundred percent. Because resource groups match app lifecycles, cost by resource group tells you exactly what each app costs.' },
    { sec: 'Cost', short: 'Common waste', title: 'The usual sources of waste',
      items: [['clock', 'Idle VMs and stopped-but-allocated VMs', 'Deallocate, do not just stop'], ['disk', 'Unattached disks and old snapshots'], ['globe', 'Unused public IPs and gateways'], ['database', 'Over-provisioned databases', 'Right-size, or use serverless']],
      tip: 'A VM stopped from inside the OS still costs money. Deallocate it in Azure.',
      n: 'Step one hundred fourteen: common waste. Idle VMs, and VMs stopped from inside the operating system, which are still allocated and still billed: deallocate them in Azure instead. Unattached disks and old snapshots. Unused public IPs and gateways. And over-provisioned databases, which serverless tiers often fix.' },
    { sec: 'Cost', short: 'Save money', title: 'Save money systematically',
      items: [['dollar', 'Reservations and savings plans', 'For steady usage'], ['zap', 'Spot VMs for batch and CI'], ['clock', 'Auto-shutdown for dev VMs', 'And scale AKS to zero at night'], ['star', 'Azure Advisor cost tab', 'Right-sizing suggestions'], ['tag', 'Tag policy + showback', 'Every team sees its bill']],
      n: 'Step one hundred fifteen: save systematically. Cover steady usage with reservations and savings plans. Use Spot VMs for batch work and CI agents. Schedule auto shutdown for development VMs, and scale non-production clusters down at night. Follow Azure Advisor\'s cost recommendations, and show every team its own bill.' },
    { sec: 'Cost', short: 'Commitments', title: 'Reservations, savings plans and Hybrid Benefit', cols: ['Option', 'Saves', 'Commit'],
      rows: [['Savings plan', 'Up to ~65%', 'Hourly spend, 1 or 3 years, any region'], ['Reservations', 'Up to ~72%', 'Specific VM size and region'], ['Azure Hybrid Benefit', 'Windows and SQL licence cost', 'Your existing licences'], ['Spot VMs', 'Up to ~90%', 'None, can be evicted']],
      tip: 'Advisor recommends right-sizing and commitments from real usage. Review it monthly.',
      n: 'Step one hundred sixteen: commitments. A savings plan for compute commits to an hourly spend for one or three years, across any region and VM size. Reservations save more, but lock a size and region. Azure Hybrid Benefit reuses existing Windows Server and SQL licences. And Spot VMs use spare capacity for up to ninety percent less. Azure Advisor recommends right sizing and commitments from real usage.' },
    { sec: 'Cost', short: 'Delete with groups', title: 'Clean up with resource groups',
      cmd: ['$ az group list --tag env=sandbox --query "[].name" -o tsv', 'rg-sandbox-kona', 'rg-sandbox-test42', '$ az group delete -n rg-sandbox-test42 --yes --no-wait', '# everything inside is removed together: no orphaned disks, IPs or NICs'],
      tip: 'Give sandbox groups an expiry tag, and clean them up on a schedule.',
      n: 'Step one hundred seventeen: clean up with resource groups. List groups by tag, like sandbox, and delete a whole experiment with one command. Everything inside goes together, with no orphaned disks, IP addresses, or network cards left behind. This is the resource group, again, being your best friend.' },
    { sec: 'Secure architectures', short: 'Secure spoke', title: 'Reference: a secure application spoke', cols: ['Subnet', 'Holds', 'Inbound from', 'Outbound via'],
      rows: [['snet-appgw', 'Application Gateway + WAF', 'Front Door only', 'App subnet'], ['snet-app / AKS', 'App Service or AKS', 'App Gateway', 'Hub firewall (UDR)'], ['snet-pe', 'PEs: SQL, KV, storage', 'App subnet', 'n/a'], ['snet-data', 'PostgreSQL Flexible', 'App subnet', 'Hub firewall']],
      tip: 'No public endpoints except Front Door. Every PaaS service behind a private endpoint.',
      n: 'Step one hundred eighteen: a secure application spoke. Front Door reaches an Application Gateway with WAF, which accepts traffic only from Front Door. Apps run in an integrated subnet or on AKS, and all outbound traffic goes through the hub firewall. SQL, Key Vault, and storage sit behind private endpoints with public access disabled. The only public entry point is Front Door.' },
    { sec: 'Secure architectures', short: 'Hub and spoke', title: 'Reference: hub-and-spoke landing zone', nodes: [['globe', 'Front Door + WAF', 'global edge'], ['shield', 'Hub VNet', 'Firewall, Bastion, ER, DNS'], ['server', 'Spokes', 'one per app and environment'], ['link', 'On-premises', 'ExpressRoute']], edges: ['Private Link origin', 'peering + UDR', ''],
      tip: 'Virtual Network Manager security admin rules enforce the must-deny rules in every spoke.',
      n: 'Step one hundred nineteen: the hub and spoke landing zone. Front Door at the edge. A hub with Azure Firewall, Bastion, ExpressRoute, and the DNS resolver, in the connectivity subscription. One spoke per application and environment, peered to the hub, with all traffic routed through the firewall. Virtual Network Manager security admin rules enforce organisation wide denies in every spoke.' },
    { sec: 'Secure architectures', short: 'Zero trust checklist', title: 'Azure secure architecture checklist', items: [['users', 'Entra ID, PIM, Conditional Access', 'Phishing-resistant MFA for admins'], ['layers', 'Landing zone policies', 'Deny public IPs, require private endpoints'], ['shield', 'Hub firewall, Bastion, no public VMs', 'Explicit egress'], ['lock', 'Managed identities everywhere', 'No secrets, no shared keys'], ['eye', 'Defender CSPM + Sentinel', 'Logs to one workspace']],
      n: 'Step one hundred twenty: the checklist to take away. Entra ID with PIM, Conditional Access, and phishing resistant MFA for admins. Landing zone policies that deny public IPs and require private endpoints. A hub firewall, Bastion, and no public virtual machines, with explicit egress. Managed identities everywhere, with shared keys and SQL passwords disabled. And Defender CSPM plus Sentinel, with logs in one workspace.' },
    { sec: 'Secure architectures', short: 'Follow a request', title: 'Follow one request end to end', nodes: [['user', 'GET /orders/42'], ['globe', 'Front Door', 'WAF, cache'], ['route', 'App Gateway', 'private link origin'], ['wheel', 'AKS pod', 'workload identity'], ['database', 'Azure SQL', 'private endpoint']], edges: ['edge', 'private', 'HTTP route', 'Entra token'],
      n: 'Step one hundred twenty one: follow one request. A customer asks for order forty two. Front Door checks the WAF rules and its cache, then reaches the Application Gateway privately. The gateway routes to an AKS pod, which uses its workload identity to get an Entra token, and queries Azure SQL through a private endpoint. No password, no public database, and every hop logged.' },
    { sec: 'Secure architectures', short: 'Delivery path', title: 'From git push to AKS', nodes: [['git', 'Pull request'], ['pipeline', 'GitHub Actions', 'OIDC to Entra'], ['box', 'ACR', 'signed image'], ['refresh', 'Flux GitOps', 'AKS extension'], ['wheel', 'AKS', 'rolling update']], edges: ['federated credential', 'push + sign', 'commit digest', 'reconcile'],
      n: 'Step one hundred twenty two: the delivery path. A pull request triggers GitHub Actions, which signs in to Azure with a federated credential, no secrets. It builds, scans, signs the image with Notation, and pushes to the container registry, then commits the new digest to the deployment repository. The Flux GitOps extension on AKS reconciles the change with a rolling update. The pipeline never holds cluster credentials.' },
    { sec: 'Cost', short: 'Learning path', title: 'Your Azure learning path',
      items: [['book', 'AZ-900 Fundamentals → AZ-104 Administrator'], ['code', 'AZ-204 Developer'], ['star', 'AZ-400 DevOps Engineer Expert'], ['rocket', 'Build: VNet → AKS / Container Apps → pipeline → Monitor', 'In a sandbox subscription with a budget']],
      n: 'Step one hundred twenty three: your learning path. Start with AZ nine hundred fundamentals, then AZ one oh four for administration, AZ two oh four for development, and AZ four hundred, the DevOps Engineer Expert. Build alongside: a VNet, an app on Container Apps or AKS, a pipeline, and monitoring, in a sandbox subscription with a budget.' },

    { type: 'mistakes', rows: [['Owner role for everyone at subscription scope', 'Least privilege, narrow scopes, PIM'], ['Secrets in app settings and pipelines', 'Key Vault + managed identities'], ['Public endpoints on PaaS services', 'Private endpoints + private DNS'], ['Random resource groups and no tags', 'Group by lifecycle; enforce tags with policy']],
      n: 'Four common mistakes. Giving everyone Owner on a whole subscription. Keeping secrets in app settings and pipelines instead of Key Vault. Leaving platform services on public endpoints. And messy resource groups without tags, which makes cost and clean up impossible.' },
    { type: 'repos', repos: [['Azure/azure-quickstart-templates', 'Thousands of ARM and Bicep samples', '', 'book'], ['Azure/bicep', 'Bicep language and examples', '', 'code'], ['Azure/Azure-Verified-Modules', 'Tested Bicep and Terraform modules', '', 'check'], ['Azure-Samples/aks-store-demo', 'Microservices demo for AKS', '', 'wheel'], ['Azure/azure-dev', 'Azure Developer CLI and templates', '', 'rocket'], ['Azure/Enterprise-Scale', 'Azure landing zone reference', '', 'layers'], ['MicrosoftDocs/azure-docs', 'Azure documentation source', '', 'file'], ['Azure-Samples/azure-search-openai-demo', 'End-to-end Azure app sample', '', 'lightbulb']],
      n: 'Practice repos: the Azure quickstart templates, Bicep, Azure Verified Modules, the AKS store demo, the Azure Developer CLI, the landing zone reference, the Azure docs, and the Azure search OpenAI demo, a complete end-to-end sample. Screenshot this frame.' },
    { type: 'recap', points: ['Entra ID, PIM, managed identities and workload identity: no secrets', 'Hub-and-spoke: firewall, Bastion, private endpoints, private DNS', 'Storage and SQL: no shared keys, Entra-only auth, soft delete, failover groups', 'AKS Automatic or a secure Standard cluster, safeguards and upgrade channels', 'Front Door + WAF, Defender CSPM, Sentinel, logs in one workspace', 'Bicep or Terraform, OIDC pipelines, GitOps, budgets and reservations'],
      n: 'Recap. Organise with management groups, subscriptions, and resource groups. Secure access with Entra ID, RBAC, and managed identities. Build private networks, then pick the right compute, from VMs to Functions. Automate with Bicep and pipelines, and watch everything with Azure Monitor. And keep the resource group as your best friend, for permissions, cost, and clean up. Follow for more, and comment AZURE if this helped.' },
  ],
};
